window.PPGUIDES={cats:[{key:"behaviour",name:"Behaviour & Mood"},{key:"essential",name:"Essential Care"},{key:"internal",name:"Internal Health"},{key:"mobility",name:"Mobility & Joint"},{key:"neuro",name:"Neurological"},{key:"paws",name:"Paws & Limbs"},{key:"rehab",name:"Rehab"},{key:"skin",name:"Skin & Allergies"}],guides:{"separation-anxiety-in-dogs":{title:"Separation Anxiety in Dogs: Helping Your Dog Cope Alone",cat:"Behaviour & Mood",catKey:"behaviour",meta:["8 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"If your dog panics, barks, chews or toilets when left alone, they may be struggling with separation anxiety. It\u2019s genuinely distressing for them (and for you), but it\u2019s also one of the most treatable behavioural problems with the right, patient approach. Here\u2019s how to help.",sections:[{h:"What is separation anxiety?",html:"<p>Separation anxiety is real distress at being left alone, not bad behaviour or 'spite'. The dog feels panicked and can\u2019t cope, which spills out as barking, destruction, toileting or pacing. Understanding it as fear, not naughtiness, is the first and most important step.</p>"},{h:"Signs your dog struggles alone",html:"<p>These typically happen soon after you leave.</p><ul><li>Barking, howling or whining when alone</li><li>Destructive behaviour, often around doors and windows</li><li>Toileting indoors despite being house-trained</li><li>Pacing, drooling or refusing food when left</li><li>Over-the-top greetings and 'shadowing' you at home</li></ul>"},{h:"Helping your dog cope",html:"<p>Build confidence gradually, there\u2019s no quick fix, but it works.</p><ul><li>Practise very short absences and build up slowly</li><li>Keep departures and returns calm and low-key</li><li>Leave them with a safe, long-lasting chew or enrichment toy</li><li>Create a cosy, secure space they associate with calm</li><li>Use calming support and consider a structured plan with a behaviourist</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Barking when alone","Destructive","Toileting indoors","Pacing"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Speak to your vet or a qualified behaviourist if your dog\u2019s distress is severe or not improving, they can rule out pain, recommend a structured behaviour programme, and discuss whether calming support or medication would help.",shop:{h:"Shop calming & anxiety support",href:"/collections/calm-anxiety"},faqTitle:"Separation Anxiety in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Can separation anxiety be cured?",a:"Many dogs improve hugely with a gradual, structured approach. Severe cases benefit from professional behavioural help and sometimes medication."},{q:"Should I get another dog to keep mine company?",a:"Not as a fix, separation anxiety is usually about missing you specifically, so a second dog often doesn\u2019t resolve it and can complicate things."},{q:"Do calming aids help?",a:"Calming support can take the edge off and help your dog learn to settle, working best alongside a gradual training plan."}],related:["dog-anxiety-and-stress","firework-noise-fear-dogs","dog-pacing-restless"]},"rehab-after-orthopaedic-surgery":{title:"Rehab After Orthopaedic Surgery in Dogs",cat:"Rehab",catKey:"rehab",meta:["7 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"If your dog has had orthopaedic surgery, a cruciate repair, fracture fixation or joint operation, the surgery itself is only part of the story. Structured rehabilitation is what rebuilds strength, restores movement and gives your dog the best possible outcome. Here\u2019s what good rehab looks like.",sections:[{h:"Why rehab matters",html:"<p>After orthopaedic surgery, muscles weaken and movement is restricted while things heal. A careful, staged rehab plan rebuilds strength and mobility, helps prevent stiffness and compensation injuries, and gets your dog safely back to normal life. Rushing it risks setbacks, so patience pays off.</p>"},{h:"The stages of recovery",html:"<p>Rehab moves through phases, always led by your vet\u2019s plan.</p><ul><li>Early rest and strict activity restriction to protect the repair</li><li>Gentle, controlled movement introduced gradually</li><li>Targeted physiotherapy to rebuild strength and flexibility</li><li>A slow, staged return to normal exercise</li><li>Hydrotherapy is often valuable in the later stages</li></ul>"},{h:"Supporting recovery at home",html:"<p>Your day-to-day care makes a big difference.</p><ul><li>Follow the activity restrictions exactly, no jumping or stairs</li><li>Use ramps and a support harness to avoid strain</li><li>Protect any wound with a recovery suit</li><li>Provide soft, supportive bedding and grippy floors</li><li>Keep to the rehab plan and attend all check-ups</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Post-surgery","Restricted rest","Rebuilding strength","Stiffness"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Always follow your surgeon\u2019s and vet\u2019s rehabilitation plan precisely, and ask about referral to a canine physiotherapist or hydrotherapist. The right, staged rehab, not rushing, gives your dog the best outcome.",shop:{h:"Shop rehab & recovery support",href:"/collections/rehabilitation"},faqTitle:"Rehab After Orthopaedic Surgery in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"How long is rehab after orthopaedic surgery?",a:"It varies by procedure but often runs over several weeks to a few months of staged, gradually increasing activity. Follow your vet\u2019s plan closely."},{q:"Why can\u2019t my dog just rest and recover?",a:"Rest is vital early on, but controlled movement and physio are what rebuild strength and prevent stiffness, recovery needs both, in the right order."},{q:"Is hydrotherapy used after surgery?",a:"Often yes, in the later stages, it builds strength with minimal joint strain. Always start it on your vet\u2019s guidance."}],related:["post-surgery-recovery-for-dogs","hydrotherapy-swimming-for-dogs","home-physiotherapy-for-dogs"]},"home-physiotherapy-for-dogs":{title:"Physiotherapy at Home for Dogs",cat:"Rehab",catKey:"rehab",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Canine physiotherapy isn\u2019t just for elite working dogs, gentle, guided exercises at home can help any dog maintain strength, flexibility and mobility, particularly during recovery or with a long-term condition. Here\u2019s how home physio can help, always under professional guidance.",sections:[{h:"What canine physio does",html:"<p>Physiotherapy uses targeted exercises and techniques to maintain and rebuild muscle, improve flexibility and coordination, ease stiffness and support recovery. A qualified canine physiotherapist assesses your dog and gives you a tailored plan to follow at home between sessions.</p>"},{h:"How it can help",html:"<p>Physio supports dogs at many stages.</p><ul><li>Rebuilding strength and movement after surgery or injury</li><li>Maintaining muscle in dogs with arthritis or mobility conditions</li><li>Improving coordination in dogs with nerve-related weakness</li><li>Keeping senior dogs supple and comfortable</li></ul>"},{h:"Doing it safely at home",html:"<p>Always work to a professional plan.</p><ul><li>Get a tailored programme from a qualified canine physiotherapist</li><li>Keep sessions short, gentle and positive</li><li>Use non-slip surfaces for any exercises</li><li>Stop if your dog seems uncomfortable and check back in</li><li>Combine with the right supportive aids and a comfy rest area</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Recovery","Stiffness","Muscle loss","Mobility issues"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Always work with your vet and a qualified canine physiotherapist before starting exercises, especially after surgery or with a health condition, the right plan, done correctly, is what makes physio safe and effective.",shop:{h:"Shop rehab & recovery support",href:"/collections/rehabilitation"},faqTitle:"Physiotherapy at Home for Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Can I do physio with my dog at home?",a:"Yes, under the guidance of a qualified canine physiotherapist, who can give you a safe, tailored plan to follow between professional sessions."},{q:"Does physio help after surgery?",a:"Very much so, guided physiotherapy helps rebuild strength and movement and supports a smoother recovery, when done to your vet\u2019s plan."},{q:"Is physio useful for arthritis?",a:"Yes, it helps maintain the muscle that supports sore joints and keeps dogs moving more comfortably."}],related:["rehab-after-orthopaedic-surgery","hydrotherapy-swimming-for-dogs","exercise-for-dogs-with-joint-problems"]},"hydrotherapy-swimming-for-dogs":{title:"Hydrotherapy and Swimming for Dogs",cat:"Rehab",catKey:"rehab",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Hydrotherapy, controlled exercise in water, is one of the most effective ways to build strength, fitness and mobility in dogs, with very little strain on the joints. It\u2019s widely used for rehab, arthritis and mobility conditions. Here\u2019s what it involves and how it can help your dog.",sections:[{h:"Why water works so well",html:"<p>In water, buoyancy supports a dog\u2019s weight, so their joints aren\u2019t loaded the way they are on land. At the same time, water provides gentle resistance that builds muscle. That combination, low impact but good effort, makes it ideal for joint and mobility problems, and for recovery after surgery.</p>"},{h:"What it helps with",html:"<p>Hydrotherapy is used for a wide range of dogs.</p><ul><li>Building strength and fitness in arthritic or older dogs</li><li>Rehabilitation after orthopaedic surgery or injury</li><li>Maintaining muscle in dogs with mobility conditions</li><li>Weight management with joint-friendly exercise</li><li>Confidence and enjoyment for water-loving dogs</li></ul>"},{h:"Getting started safely",html:"<p>Do it properly to keep it safe and effective.</p><ul><li>Use a qualified canine hydrotherapist where possible</li><li>A float or buoyancy vest helps nervous or weaker dogs</li><li>Build up sessions gradually</li><li>Always follow your vet\u2019s guidance, especially post-surgery</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Arthritis","Recovery","Low fitness","Mobility issues"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Always check with your vet before starting hydrotherapy, particularly after surgery or with a health condition. They can refer you to a qualified canine hydrotherapist and advise what\u2019s suitable.",shop:{h:"Shop rehab & hydrotherapy support",href:"/collections/rehabilitation"},faqTitle:"Hydrotherapy and Swimming for Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Is swimming good for dogs with arthritis?",a:"Yes, it builds strength and fitness with minimal joint strain, which is why hydrotherapy is so often recommended for arthritic and older dogs."},{q:"Do I need a professional hydrotherapist?",a:"For rehab and dogs with health conditions, a qualified canine hydrotherapist is ideal. A buoyancy vest helps support nervous or weaker swimmers."},{q:"Can any dog do hydrotherapy?",a:"Many can benefit, but always check with your vet first, some conditions need specific precautions, especially after surgery."}],related:["exercise-for-dogs-with-joint-problems","rehab-after-orthopaedic-surgery","dog-arthritis-signs-and-support"]},"nerve-weakness-in-dogs":{title:"Supporting a Dog with Nerve-Related Weakness",cat:"Neurological",catKey:"neuro",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Not all weakness comes from sore joints, sometimes the problem lies in the nerves that carry signals to the legs. Nerve-related weakness needs a slightly different approach, focused on protection, support and maintaining what strength and coordination your dog has. Here\u2019s a practical overview.",sections:[{h:"How nerve weakness differs",html:"<p>When nerves aren\u2019t carrying signals properly, a dog may know they want to move a leg but can\u2019t do it accurately, leading to knuckling, scuffing, wobbliness and weakness. Joints and muscles may be fine; the issue is the 'wiring'. Conditions like degenerative myelopathy and some spinal problems work this way.</p>"},{h:"Signs of nerve-related weakness",html:"<p>The pattern is often coordination-based.</p><ul><li>Knuckling, paws dragging or turning under</li><li>Wobbliness or a swaying, uncoordinated gait</li><li>Scuffed nails and grazes on the tops of the paws</li><li>Gradual loss of strength, often in the back legs</li></ul>"},{h:"How to support your dog",html:"<p>Protect, assist and keep moving.</p><ul><li>Anti-knuckling boots to protect dragging paws</li><li>A support harness to help with balance and walking</li><li>Non-slip flooring and ramps for safety</li><li>Gentle, regular exercise and physiotherapy to maintain muscle</li><li>Careful skin and paw checks to catch grazes early</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Knuckling","Wobbliness","Scuffed paws","Gradual weakness"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Always have nerve-related weakness assessed by your vet to identify the cause and rule out treatable conditions. They and a physiotherapist can guide the best support and exercise plan for your dog.",shop:{h:"Shop nerve & mobility support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Supporting a Dog with Nerve-Related Weakness, answered",faq:[{q:"How is nerve weakness different from joint weakness?",a:"Joint weakness comes from pain or wear in the joints; nerve weakness comes from problems with the signals controlling the legs, often causing knuckling and poor coordination."},{q:"Can physiotherapy help nerve weakness?",a:"Yes, gentle physiotherapy and regular movement help maintain muscle, circulation and coordination, supporting quality of life."},{q:"Do anti-knuckling boots help?",a:"They protect paws that drag and can help with foot placement, improving comfort and confidence on walks."}],related:["degenerative-myelopathy-in-dogs","knuckling-in-dogs"]},"dog-wheelchairs-mobility-aids":{title:"Paralysis and Mobility Loss in Dogs",cat:"Neurological",catKey:"neuro",meta:["7 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Losing the use of their legs, whether suddenly or gradually, is one of the most distressing things that can happen to a dog and their owner. But with veterinary care and the right support, a remarkable number of dogs adapt and go on to live happy, full lives. Here\u2019s an overview of causes and support.",sections:[{h:"Causes of mobility loss",html:"<p>Paralysis or partial paralysis can result from spinal conditions like IVDD, nerve diseases such as degenerative myelopathy, injuries, or other neurological problems. Sudden loss of movement is an emergency; gradual loss still needs prompt veterinary assessment to identify the cause.</p>"},{h:"Living well with reduced mobility",html:"<p>Day-to-day support focuses on dignity, comfort and getting around.</p><ul><li>Support harnesses to assist standing and walking</li><li>Wheelchairs (carts) that restore independent movement</li><li>Anti-knuckling boots to protect dragging paws</li><li>Ramps and non-slip flooring throughout the home</li><li>Help with toileting, hygiene and pressure-sore prevention</li></ul>"},{h:"Keeping comfortable",html:"<p>Comfort and skin care become really important.</p><ul><li>Soft, supportive bedding and regular position changes</li><li>Keeping the skin clean and dry to prevent sores</li><li>Gentle physiotherapy to maintain muscle and circulation</li><li>Plenty of mental enrichment and affection</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Dragging legs","Can\u2019t stand","Knuckling","Needs support"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Sudden loss of movement is an emergency, contact your vet immediately. For gradual mobility loss, prompt assessment identifies the cause and the best support. Your vet and a physiotherapist can guide you on aids and care.",shop:{h:"Shop mobility aids & support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Paralysis and Mobility Loss in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Can a paralysed dog have a good quality of life?",a:"Many do, with the right support, aids like wheelchairs, good nursing care and lots of love, a great number of dogs adapt remarkably well."},{q:"What is a dog wheelchair?",a:"A wheeled cart that supports the back (or front) end, letting a dog with reduced mobility move around independently and stay active."},{q:"Is sudden paralysis an emergency?",a:"Yes, sudden loss of movement needs immediate veterinary attention, as prompt treatment greatly improves the outlook."}],related:["degenerative-myelopathy-in-dogs","dog-wheelchairs-mobility-aids","ivdd-in-dogs"]},"dog-wheelchairs-and-mobility-aids-a-guide":{title:"Dog Wheelchairs and Mobility Aids: A Guide",cat:"Neurological",catKey:"neuro",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"When a dog\u2019s legs no longer work as they should, the right mobility aids can give them back their freedom, and their joy. From simple support harnesses to full wheelchairs, there\u2019s a lot that can help. Here\u2019s a friendly overview of the options and when each helps.",sections:[{h:"The range of mobility aids",html:"<p>Different aids suit different needs and stages, and they often work together.</p><ul><li>Support harnesses, gently take weight and help with stairs, getting up and walks</li><li>Wheelchairs (carts), restore independent movement for dogs with weak or paralysed back legs</li><li>Anti-knuckling boots, protect paws that drag or turn under</li><li>Ramps, remove the need to jump into cars or onto furniture</li><li>Non-slip mats, give grip and confidence on hard floors</li></ul>"},{h:"Choosing the right aid",html:"<p>It depends on your dog\u2019s mobility and the cause.</p><ul><li>Mild weakness, a support harness and grippy floors may be enough</li><li>Significant back-leg weakness or paralysis, a wheelchair restores movement</li><li>Dragging paws, add protective boots</li><li>Always get the fit and measurements right for comfort and safety</li></ul>"},{h:"Getting started",html:"<p>A little patience helps your dog adapt.</p><ul><li>Introduce a new aid gradually and positively</li><li>Build up wheelchair time slowly at first</li><li>Check regularly for rubbing or pressure points</li><li>Ask your vet or physiotherapist for fitting advice</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Weak back legs","Dragging","Can\u2019t walk far","Needs support"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Your vet or a canine physiotherapist can advise which aids will help your dog most and how to fit them correctly, the right fit makes all the difference to comfort and safety.",shop:{h:"Shop wheelchairs & mobility aids",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Dog Wheelchairs and Mobility Aids, answered",faq:[{q:"When does a dog need a wheelchair?",a:"When their back (or sometimes front) legs can no longer reliably support them, a wheelchair restores safe, independent movement and quality of life."},{q:"Will my dog get used to a wheelchair?",a:"Most dogs adapt quickly, especially when introduced gradually and positively, many are visibly delighted to move freely again."},{q:"Can mobility aids be used together?",a:"Yes, harnesses, boots, ramps and wheelchairs often work brilliantly together depending on your dog\u2019s needs."}],related:["degenerative-myelopathy-in-dogs","rear-leg-weakness-in-dogs"]},"dog-after-spay-neuter":{title:"Caring for Your Dog After Spay or Neuter",cat:"Essential Care",catKey:"essential",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Spaying and neutering are among the most common operations dogs have, but they\u2019re still surgery, and the recovery period matters. A calm, comfortable dog that leaves the wound alone heals quickly and uneventfully. Here\u2019s how to get your dog through it smoothly.",sections:[{h:"What to expect",html:"<p>Most dogs bounce back surprisingly quickly, but the wound needs around 10 to 14 days to heal properly. The biggest risks come from too much activity too soon, and from licking or chewing the incision. Your vet\u2019s specific aftercare instructions always come first.</p>"},{h:"The first two weeks",html:"<p>Calm, protected and rested is the recipe.</p><ul><li>Restrict activity, no running, jumping or rough play</li><li>Protect the wound with a recovery suit or cone</li><li>Keep the incision clean and dry, and check it daily</li><li>Short, calm lead walks only for toileting</li><li>Give any medication exactly as prescribed</li></ul>"},{h:"Watching the wound",html:"<p>Know what\u2019s normal and what isn\u2019t.</p><ul><li>A little redness and bruising early on can be normal</li><li>Watch for increasing swelling, discharge, heat or a bad smell</li><li>Make sure stitches stay intact</li><li>Stop your dog licking the area at all costs</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Recent surgery","Stitches","Licking incision","Restricted rest"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Contact your vet if the incision looks increasingly red, swollen, hot or is discharging, if stitches come loose, or if your dog seems unusually painful, off their food or lethargic. Quick action keeps recovery on track.",shop:{h:"Shop recovery & wound care",href:"/collections/recovery-immunity"},faqTitle:"Caring for Your Dog After Spay or Neuter, answered",faq:[{q:"How long does spay/neuter recovery take?",a:"Usually around 10 to 14 days for the wound to heal. Follow your vet\u2019s timeline and keep activity restricted throughout."},{q:"Does my dog need a cone after being neutered?",a:"They need something to stop licking the wound, a cone or a recovery suit. Many dogs find a soft suit more comfortable."},{q:"When can my dog exercise normally again?",a:"Only once your vet is happy the wound has healed, usually after the post-op check. Returning to activity too soon risks complications."}],related:["post-surgery-recovery-for-dogs","recovery-suits-vs-cones","wound-care-for-dogs"]},"dog-dental-routine":{title:"Building a Simple Dental Routine for Your Dog",cat:"Essential Care",catKey:"essential",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Dental disease is one of the most common health problems in dogs, and one of the most preventable. The secret isn\u2019t a one-off deep clean, it\u2019s a simple, consistent home routine. Here\u2019s how to build one your dog will actually accept.",sections:[{h:"Why a routine beats a one-off",html:"<p>Plaque starts forming within hours of eating and hardens into stubborn tartar within days. That\u2019s why little-and-often care, ideally daily brushing, keeps teeth far healthier than the occasional big effort. A routine prevents problems rather than chasing them.</p>"},{h:"Building the habit",html:"<p>Go slowly and keep it positive.</p><ul><li>Start by letting your dog lick dog toothpaste off your finger</li><li>Progress to gently touching the teeth and gums</li><li>Introduce a soft brush or finger brush a few teeth at a time</li><li>Always finish on a positive note with praise</li><li>Aim for daily, but a few times a week still helps a lot</li></ul>"},{h:"Supporting the routine",html:"<p>Brushing is best, with extras to back it up.</p><ul><li>Use dog toothpaste, never human toothpaste</li><li>Add dental chews and toys to help reduce plaque</li><li>Check the mouth regularly so you spot changes early</li><li>Book routine dental check-ups with your vet</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Bad breath","Tartar","Sore gums","Dropping food"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if you notice heavy tartar, red or bleeding gums, loose teeth or signs of mouth pain, established tartar needs a professional clean, after which a home routine keeps things healthy.",shop:{h:"Shop dental care",href:"/collections/dental-care"},faqTitle:"Building a Simple Dental Routine for Your Dog, answered",faq:[{q:"How often should I brush my dog\u2019s teeth?",a:"Daily is ideal, but even a few times a week makes a real difference. Consistency matters more than perfection."},{q:"My dog hates having their teeth brushed, what can I do?",a:"Build up very gradually and keep it positive, starting with just tasting the toothpaste. Dental chews help in the meantime."},{q:"Do dental chews work?",a:"They\u2019re a useful addition that helps reduce plaque, but they work best alongside brushing rather than instead of it."}],related:["dental-disease-in-dogs","senior-dog-care-guide"]},"recovery-suits-vs-cones":{title:"Recovery Suits vs Cones: Which Is Better for Your Dog?",cat:"Essential Care",catKey:"essential",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"After surgery or an injury, stopping your dog reaching the wound is essential, but the traditional plastic cone is famously unpopular. Recovery suits offer a comfier alternative for many situations. Here\u2019s how the two compare so you can choose the right one for your dog.",sections:[{h:"Why protection matters",html:"<p>Licking and chewing introduce bacteria and can pull stitches, undoing the healing and risking infection. Whatever method you choose, the goal is the same: keep your dog from interfering with the wound while it heals.</p>"},{h:"How they compare",html:"<p>Each has its place depending on the wound.</p><ul><li>Recovery suits cover body and limb wounds while letting dogs eat, drink, sleep and move comfortably</li><li>Cones protect wounds anywhere, including the head and face, but many dogs find them stressful and bump into things</li><li>Suits are washable and reusable; cones are simple and cheap</li><li>Some situations (like facial wounds) genuinely need a cone</li></ul>"},{h:"Choosing the right one",html:"<p>Match it to the wound and your dog.</p><ul><li>Body, tummy or limb wound, a recovery suit is often ideal</li><li>Head, face or ear wound, a cone is usually needed</li><li>An anxious dog who hates the cone, a suit may reduce stress</li><li>Always check it fully prevents access to the wound</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Post-surgery","Healing wound","Licking","Stitches"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Always follow your vet\u2019s advice on protecting a specific wound, they\u2019ll tell you what the surgical site needs. If your dog is distressed by a cone, ask whether a recovery suit would work for their wound.",shop:{h:"Shop recovery suits & care",href:"/collections/recovery-immunity"},faqTitle:"Recovery Suits vs Cones, answered",faq:[{q:"Are recovery suits as effective as cones?",a:"For body and limb wounds, a well-fitted recovery suit protects the area effectively while being far more comfortable. Facial wounds usually still need a cone."},{q:"Can my dog wear a recovery suit all day?",a:"Yes, they\u2019re designed to be worn comfortably, including for eating, drinking, sleeping and gentle toilet breaks. Check the fit and keep it clean."},{q:"Which is less stressful for my dog?",a:"Most dogs find a soft recovery suit far less stressful than a rigid cone, which can be a real help for anxious dogs."}],related:["post-surgery-recovery-for-dogs","wound-care-for-dogs","dog-after-spay-neuter"]},"wound-care-for-dogs":{title:"Wound Care for Dogs at Home",cat:"Essential Care",catKey:"essential",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Cuts, grazes and small wounds are part and parcel of an active dog\u2019s life. Knowing how to clean and protect a minor wound at home, support healing, and recognise when something needs the vet, is genuinely useful knowledge for every owner. Here\u2019s a practical guide.",sections:[{h:"First steps with a minor wound",html:"<p>For small, clean grazes and cuts, gentle care at home is usually enough. The priorities are to clean the area, stop your dog making it worse, and keep an eye on healing. Anything deep, bleeding heavily or in a tricky spot needs the vet.</p>"},{h:"Caring for a healing wound",html:"<p>Clean, protected and undisturbed is the goal.</p><ul><li>Gently clean the area and keep it clean and dry</li><li>Stop licking and chewing, a recovery suit beats a cone</li><li>Check daily for redness, swelling, heat, discharge or smell</li><li>Keep your dog calm and limit activity while it heals</li></ul>"},{h:"Helping it heal well",html:"<p>A little support goes a long way.</p><ul><li>A soft recovery suit or appropriate dressing to protect the area</li><li>A clean, comfortable rest space</li><li>Soothing care for any surrounding irritation</li><li>Follow any wound care instructions from your vet exactly</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Cut or graze","Licking the wound","Redness","Swelling"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet for any wound that is deep, bleeding heavily, gaping, dirty, near the eyes, or showing signs of infection (redness, swelling, heat, discharge or smell). When in doubt, get it checked, small problems treated early stay small.",shop:{h:"Shop wound care & recovery",href:"/collections/recovery-immunity"},faqTitle:"Wound Care for Dogs at Home, answered",faq:[{q:"Can I clean my dog\u2019s wound myself?",a:"For small, clean grazes, gentle cleaning and protection at home is usually fine. Deeper or infected wounds need veterinary care."},{q:"Should I use a cone or a recovery suit?",a:"A soft recovery suit is often comfier and less stressful than a cone for body and limb wounds, while still stopping licking."},{q:"How do I know if a wound is infected?",a:"Watch for increasing redness, swelling, heat, discharge or a bad smell, these mean it\u2019s time to see your vet."}],related:["post-surgery-recovery-for-dogs","recovery-suits-vs-cones"]},"dog-pacing-restless":{title:"Why Is My Dog Pacing and Restless?",cat:"Behaviour & Mood",catKey:"behaviour",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"A dog that paces, circles or just can\u2019t seem to settle is often a worried or uncomfortable dog. Restlessness is a signal worth paying attention to, it can be behavioural, but sometimes it points to discomfort or a health issue. Here\u2019s how to think it through.",sections:[{h:"Common causes of restlessness",html:"<p>Pacing and an inability to settle most often come from stress or anxiety, but they can also reflect pain or discomfort, needing the toilet, too little physical or mental exercise, or, in older dogs, age-related cognitive changes.</p>"},{h:"What to look for",html:"<p>Notice the context and any other signs.</p><ul><li>Pacing, circling or repeatedly resettling</li><li>Unable to relax even in a calm environment</li><li>Panting or whining without an obvious cause</li><li>Restlessness that\u2019s worse at night</li><li>Other changes, appetite, toileting or behaviour</li></ul>"},{h:"How to help",html:"<p>Cover the basics, then support calm.</p><ul><li>Make sure they\u2019ve had enough exercise and enrichment</li><li>Create a calm, comfortable, predictable environment</li><li>Give a safe space to settle in</li><li>Use calming support for anxious dogs</li><li>Rule out discomfort, especially in older dogs</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Pacing","Can\u2019t settle","Panting","Worse at night"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if restlessness is new, persistent, or comes with other changes, pain and several medical conditions can cause it, and it\u2019s important to rule these out, especially in senior dogs.",shop:{h:"Shop calming support",href:"/collections/calm-anxiety"},faqTitle:"Why Is My Dog Pacing and Restless?, answered",faq:[{q:"Why is my dog suddenly restless at night?",a:"Night-time restlessness can signal discomfort, anxiety or, in older dogs, cognitive changes. If it\u2019s new or persistent, it\u2019s worth a vet check."},{q:"Could pacing mean my dog is in pain?",a:"Yes, discomfort is a common and easily missed cause of restlessness, so it\u2019s always worth ruling out, particularly in senior dogs."},{q:"Do calming aids help a restless dog?",a:"For anxious dogs, calming support can help them settle, alongside enough exercise, a calm environment and ruling out any physical cause."}],related:["dog-anxiety-and-stress","separation-anxiety-in-dogs","senior-dog-care-guide"]},"reactive-dog-calming":{title:"Calming an Over-Excited or Reactive Dog",cat:"Behaviour & Mood",catKey:"behaviour",meta:["7 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"A dog that lunges, barks or 'loses the plot' at other dogs, people or movement is often over-aroused and struggling to cope, rather than being aggressive. Reactivity is common and manageable with patience, the right techniques and support. Here\u2019s how to help your dog feel, and behave, calmer.",sections:[{h:"Understanding reactivity",html:"<p>Reactivity is an over-the-top response to a trigger, often fear, frustration or over-excitement. The dog\u2019s arousal level spikes so high they can\u2019t think or respond to you. The aim of management isn\u2019t punishment; it\u2019s helping them stay below the threshold where they tip over.</p>"},{h:"What it can look like",html:"<p>Reactivity has a recognisable pattern around triggers.</p><ul><li>Lunging, barking or spinning at other dogs, people or traffic</li><li>Becoming 'glued' to a trigger and unable to focus on you</li><li>Struggling to settle after an exciting or stressful event</li><li>Pulling hard and a generally high state of arousal</li></ul>"},{h:"Helping a reactive dog",html:"<p>Calm, distance and consistency are your tools.</p><ul><li>Create distance from triggers so your dog stays under threshold</li><li>Reward calm focus on you with high-value treats</li><li>Keep walks predictable and avoid flooding them with triggers</li><li>Make sure they get enough rest, tired dogs are more reactive</li><li>Use calming support and consider working with a behaviourist</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Lunging","Barking at triggers","Can\u2019t settle","Pulling hard"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> A qualified, reward-based behaviourist is invaluable for reactivity. Your vet can also rule out pain (a common hidden driver) and discuss whether calming support would help your dog learn more easily.",shop:{h:"Shop calming support",href:"/collections/calm-anxiety"},faqTitle:"Calming an Over-Excited or Reactive Dog, answered",faq:[{q:"Is my reactive dog aggressive?",a:"Often not, reactivity is usually about fear, frustration or over-excitement rather than a desire to harm. A behaviourist can help you understand your dog\u2019s triggers."},{q:"Can rest really reduce reactivity?",a:"Yes, over-tired, over-stimulated dogs are far more reactive. Plenty of good-quality rest genuinely helps."},{q:"Do calming aids help reactive dogs?",a:"Calming support can lower baseline arousal so your dog finds it easier to learn and cope, alongside a reward-based training plan."}],related:["dog-anxiety-and-stress","dog-pacing-restless","firework-noise-fear-dogs"]},"firework-noise-fear-dogs":{title:"Helping Dogs with Firework and Noise Fear",cat:"Behaviour & Mood",catKey:"behaviour",meta:["7 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"For a lot of dogs, fireworks season is genuinely frightening. Noise phobias are common, and a scared dog can\u2019t simply be told not to worry. But with preparation and the right support, you can make loud nights far less distressing. Here\u2019s how.",sections:[{h:"Why noises frighten dogs",html:"<p>Dogs have far more sensitive hearing than us, and sudden, unpredictable loud noises like fireworks or thunder can trigger a genuine fear response. Some dogs are naturally more sensitive, and fear can build over time if scary experiences aren\u2019t managed.</p>"},{h:"Signs of noise fear",html:"<p>Fear shows up physically and behaviourally.</p><ul><li>Trembling, panting or drooling</li><li>Hiding, pacing or trying to escape</li><li>Clinginess or seeking reassurance</li><li>Barking, whining or refusing to go outside</li><li>Destructive behaviour in a panic</li></ul>"},{h:"Preparing and helping",html:"<p>Set up a safe haven and plan ahead.</p><ul><li>Create a cosy den where your dog feels secure</li><li>Close curtains and put on background noise to muffle bangs</li><li>Stay calm and reassuring, never punish fearful behaviour</li><li>Use calming aids or a pressure vest, starting before the noise</li><li>Walk and toilet before dark on firework nights</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Trembling","Hiding","Pacing","Panting"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> If your dog\u2019s noise fear is severe or worsening, speak to your vet or a behaviourist ahead of firework season, they can advise on a longer-term desensitisation plan and, where needed, medication that calming aids work alongside.",shop:{h:"Shop calming support & vests",href:"/collections/calm-anxiety"},faqTitle:"Helping Dogs with Firework and Noise Fear, answered",faq:[{q:"How can I calm my dog during fireworks?",a:"Create a safe den, muffle the noise, stay calm, and use calming support started before the fireworks begin. Planning ahead makes a big difference."},{q:"Should I comfort a frightened dog?",a:"Yes, comforting a scared dog won\u2019t 'reward' the fear. Calm reassurance and a secure space help them cope. Never punish fearful behaviour."},{q:"Do pressure vests work?",a:"Many dogs find the gentle, constant pressure reassuring during stressful events like fireworks, especially alongside a calm environment."}],related:["dog-anxiety-and-stress","separation-anxiety-in-dogs","reactive-dog-calming"]},"yeast-infections-smelly-skin-dogs":{title:"Yeast Infections and Smelly Skin in Dogs",cat:"Skin & Allergies",catKey:"skin",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"If your dog has developed a distinctive musty or 'corn chip' smell, along with greasy, itchy skin, a yeast overgrowth could be the culprit. Yeast lives naturally on the skin, but certain conditions let it multiply, and the result is uncomfortable and smelly. Here\u2019s how to tackle it.",sections:[{h:"What is a yeast overgrowth?",html:"<p>Yeast is normally present on healthy skin in small amounts. Problems start when something, often allergies, moisture or a warm skin fold, lets it overgrow. It tends to affect the paws, ears, armpits, skin folds and belly, causing itching, greasiness and that telltale smell.</p>"},{h:"Signs to look for",html:"<p>The combination of smell and itch is a strong clue.</p><ul><li>A musty, yeasty or 'corn chip' smell</li><li>Greasy, flaky or thickened skin</li><li>Intense itching, licking and chewing</li><li>Brown staining on the fur, especially on the paws</li><li>Recurring ear problems</li></ul>"},{h:"Managing yeasty skin",html:"<p>Keep skin clean and dry, and tackle the trigger.</p><ul><li>Keep skin folds and paws clean and thoroughly dry</li><li>Use soothing, anti-itch topical care on affected areas</li><li>Address underlying allergies, a very common trigger</li><li>Support the skin barrier with omega oils</li><li>Don\u2019t let moisture linger after baths or swimming</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Musty smell","Greasy skin","Itching","Stained fur"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet to confirm a yeast infection and get the right treatment, overgrowths often need specific medicated products, and recurring cases point to an underlying cause like allergies worth addressing.",shop:{h:"Shop skin & coat care",href:"/collections/skin-coat"},faqTitle:"Yeast Infections and Smelly Skin in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Why does my dog smell musty?",a:"A musty or 'corn chip' smell with greasy, itchy skin often points to a yeast overgrowth, which your vet can confirm and treat."},{q:"Is yeast infection linked to allergies?",a:"Very often, allergies disrupt the skin and let yeast overgrow, which is why addressing the underlying allergy helps prevent recurrence."},{q:"How do I stop it coming back?",a:"Keep skin and folds clean and dry, support the skin barrier, and work with your vet to manage the underlying cause."}],related:["itchy-skin-allergies-in-dogs","ear-infections-in-dogs","dry-flaky-skin-dandruff-dogs"]},"ear-infections-in-dogs":{title:"Ear Infections in Dogs: Signs and Care",cat:"Skin & Allergies",catKey:"skin",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Ear infections are one of the most common reasons dogs visit the vet, and they\u2019re often linked to allergies. They\u2019re itchy, sore and tend to recur, but with prompt treatment and good routine care, you can keep your dog\u2019s ears healthy and comfortable.",sections:[{h:"Why dogs get ear problems",html:"<p>A dog\u2019s ear canal is long and L-shaped, which traps moisture and wax, perfect conditions for yeast and bacteria. Allergies are a very common underlying trigger, as are moisture from swimming, grass seeds, and ear shape (floppy-eared breeds are more prone).</p>"},{h:"Signs of an ear infection",html:"<p>Ears that are bothering your dog are usually obvious once you know the signs.</p><ul><li>Head shaking or tilting</li><li>Scratching or pawing at the ears</li><li>An unpleasant smell from the ears</li><li>Red, inflamed or waxy ear flaps and canals</li><li>Pain when the ears are touched</li></ul>"},{h:"Keeping ears healthy",html:"<p>Prevention and gentle routine care reduce recurrence.</p><ul><li>Dry ears thoroughly after swimming or baths</li><li>Check ears regularly, especially in floppy-eared breeds</li><li>Keep the fur around the ear opening tidy</li><li>Address underlying allergies, a common root cause</li><li>Avoid poking anything deep into the ear canal</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Head shaking","Scratching ears","Odour","Red ear flaps"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet for a suspected ear infection, they need the right cleaning and medication, and it\u2019s important not to use leftover or human products. Recurrent infections usually point to an underlying cause like allergies worth investigating.",shop:{h:"Shop ear & skin care",href:"/collections/skin-coat"},faqTitle:"Ear Infections in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Why does my dog keep getting ear infections?",a:"Recurrent infections usually have an underlying cause, most often allergies. Addressing that, alongside good ear care, reduces flare-ups."},{q:"Can I treat an ear infection at home?",a:"Routine cleaning and drying help prevent problems, but an active infection needs your vet\u2019s diagnosis and the right medication."},{q:"Should I clean my dog\u2019s ears regularly?",a:"Gentle, routine ear care helps prevent problems, especially in floppy-eared dogs and swimmers, but never poke deep into the canal."}],related:["itchy-skin-allergies-in-dogs","seasonal-allergies-in-dogs","yeast-infections-smelly-skin-dogs"]},"omega-oils-for-dogs-skin-coat-and-beyond":{title:"Omega Oils for Dogs: Skin, Coat and Beyond",cat:"Skin & Allergies",catKey:"skin",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"If your dog has dry, itchy skin or a dull coat, omega oils are one of the most popular and well-regarded supplements to reach for. But they do more than just add shine. Here\u2019s what omega oils are, what they help with, and how to use them well.",sections:[{h:"What are omega oils?",html:"<p>Omega-3 and omega-6 are essential fatty acids, fats your dog needs from their diet because they can\u2019t make enough themselves. They\u2019re building blocks for healthy skin, and they play a role in managing inflammation, which is why they\u2019re so often recommended for itchy, irritated skin.</p>"},{h:"What they help with",html:"<p>The benefits go beyond a glossy coat.</p><ul><li>Supporting the skin barrier to reduce dryness and irritation</li><li>Improving coat condition and shine</li><li>Helping to calm itchy, inflamed skin as part of a wider plan</li><li>Supporting joints, heart and general wellbeing</li></ul>"},{h:"Using omega oils well",html:"<p>Consistency is key with any supplement.</p><ul><li>Choose a quality omega supplement made for dogs</li><li>Give consistently, benefits build over several weeks</li><li>Use alongside a balanced diet</li><li>Introduce gradually to let the tummy adjust</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Dry skin","Dull coat","Itching","Flaky skin"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> If your dog\u2019s skin is very itchy, red, broken or smelly, see your vet, omega oils support healthy skin, but underlying allergies or infections need proper treatment too.",shop:{h:"Shop omega & coat support",href:"/collections/skin-coat"},faqTitle:"Omega Oils for Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"How long do omega oils take to work?",a:"Skin and coat benefits usually build over several weeks of consistent use, so give them time."},{q:"Can omega oils help itchy skin?",a:"They support the skin barrier and can help calm irritation as part of a wider plan, alongside addressing the underlying cause."},{q:"Are omega oils good for joints too?",a:"Omega-3s in particular support joints and general wellbeing, which is a nice added bonus of using them for skin."}],related:["itchy-skin-allergies-in-dogs","dry-flaky-skin-dandruff-dogs","seasonal-allergies-in-dogs"]},"seasonal-allergies-in-dogs":{title:"Seasonal Allergies in Dogs: Spotting and Soothing the Itch",cat:"Skin & Allergies",catKey:"skin",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Does your dog start scratching every spring or summer? Seasonal allergies, usually to pollen, are extremely common, and they can make life miserable for itchy dogs. While there\u2019s no quick cure, a consistent routine keeps the itch under control through the worst months.",sections:[{h:"What are seasonal allergies?",html:"<p>Just like people with hay fever, dogs can react to airborne allergens like grass, tree and weed pollens that peak at certain times of year. Instead of sneezing, dogs usually show it through their skin, itching, licking and irritation, often around the paws, face, ears and tummy.</p>"},{h:"Signs of seasonal allergies",html:"<p>The timing is the biggest clue.</p><ul><li>Itching that flares up at the same time each year</li><li>Licking or chewing paws, especially after walks</li><li>Red, irritated skin and recurring ear problems</li><li>Rubbing the face or scooting</li></ul>"},{h:"Managing the itch",html:"<p>Reduce exposure and soothe the skin.</p><ul><li>Wipe down the coat and paws after walks to remove pollen</li><li>Use soothing topical products on irritated areas</li><li>Support the skin barrier with omega oils</li><li>Keep on top of flea prevention to avoid stacking up triggers</li><li>Stay consistent through the whole season, not just during flare-ups</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Seasonal itching","Paw licking","Red skin","Face rubbing"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if the itching is intense, the skin is broken or infected, or home care isn\u2019t keeping things comfortable, allergies often benefit from a tailored treatment plan that supportive products work alongside.",shop:{h:"Shop allergy & skin support",href:"/collections/skin-coat"},faqTitle:"Seasonal Allergies in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Can dogs get hay fever?",a:"Effectively yes, dogs can be allergic to pollens, but they usually show it through itchy skin rather than sneezing."},{q:"How do I reduce my dog\u2019s pollen exposure?",a:"Wipe the coat and paws after walks, avoid peak-pollen times where you can, and keep a consistent skin routine through the season."},{q:"Will the allergy go away?",a:"Seasonal allergies tend to recur each year, but a consistent management routine keeps most dogs comfortable through the worst of it."}],related:["itchy-skin-allergies-in-dogs","dog-licking-paws"]},"dry-flaky-skin-dandruff-dogs":{title:"Dry, Flaky Skin and Dandruff in Dogs",cat:"Skin & Allergies",catKey:"skin",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Spotting flakes of dandruff in your dog\u2019s coat, or noticing dry, dull skin, is common, and usually very treatable. It can be down to anything from dry air to diet or an underlying skin issue. Here\u2019s how to get to the bottom of it and bring back a healthy, shiny coat.",sections:[{h:"Why skin gets dry and flaky",html:"<p>Dry, flaky skin happens when the skin\u2019s natural barrier isn\u2019t working as well as it should. Common causes include low humidity (especially in winter), over-bathing, a diet lacking the right fats, allergies, parasites, or an underlying skin condition.</p>"},{h:"Signs to look for",html:"<p>Look beyond the flakes to the skin and coat overall.</p><ul><li>White flakes in the coat or where your dog has been lying</li><li>Dry, dull or rough-feeling coat</li><li>Mild itching or scratching</li><li>Patches of flaky or scaly skin</li></ul>"},{h:"Restoring healthy skin",html:"<p>Support the skin barrier from inside and out.</p><ul><li>Add omega oils to support skin and coat condition</li><li>Avoid over-bathing, which strips natural oils</li><li>Use gentle, moisturising grooming products</li><li>Groom regularly to distribute natural oils</li><li>Make sure the diet provides good-quality fats</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["White flakes","Dull coat","Mild itching","Scaly patches"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if the skin is very itchy, red or smelly, the dandruff is heavy or sudden, or there are bald patches, these can point to allergies, parasites or infection that need treating.",shop:{h:"Shop skin & coat care",href:"/collections/skin-coat"},faqTitle:"Dry, Flaky Skin and Dandruff in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Why does my dog have dandruff?",a:"Common causes include dry air, over-bathing, a diet low in good fats, allergies or parasites. Omega support and gentle grooming often help."},{q:"Do omega oils help dry skin?",a:"Yes, omega-3 and -6 fatty acids support the skin barrier and coat condition, reducing dryness and flaking over time."},{q:"Should I bathe my flaky dog more often?",a:"Usually less, not more, over-bathing strips natural oils. Use gentle, moisturising products and don\u2019t over-do it."}],related:["itchy-skin-allergies-in-dogs","seasonal-allergies-in-dogs"]},"sensitive-stomach-dogs":{title:"Sensitive Stomachs in Dogs: A Practical Guide",cat:"Internal Health",catKey:"internal",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Some dogs just have delicate digestion, prone to loose stools, wind or sickness at the slightest change. A sensitive stomach is frustrating, but it\u2019s usually very manageable once you understand the triggers and build some steady routines. Here\u2019s how to settle things down.",sections:[{h:"What causes a sensitive stomach?",html:"<p>Some dogs are naturally more sensitive; others react to specific ingredients, sudden diet changes, rich treats, stress, or scavenging. An imbalance in the gut\u2019s natural bacteria can keep the cycle going, which is why consistency matters so much.</p>"},{h:"Signs of a sensitive tummy",html:"<p>These tend to flare up around triggers.</p><ul><li>Frequent loose stools or diarrhoea</li><li>Excessive wind</li><li>Occasional vomiting or regurgitation</li><li>A gurgling, noisy stomach</li><li>Picky or variable appetite</li></ul>"},{h:"Settling the gut",html:"<p>Consistency is everything with a sensitive stomach.</p><ul><li>Feed a consistent, easily digestible diet</li><li>Change foods slowly over a week or more</li><li>Avoid rich treats and scavenged scraps</li><li>Support the gut with a probiotic</li><li>Keep a food-and-symptom diary to spot patterns</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Loose stools","Wind","Picky appetite","Noisy tummy"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if upsets are frequent, severe, contain blood, or your dog is losing weight, ongoing digestive problems sometimes need investigating and treating beyond home management.",shop:{h:"Shop digestion & gut support",href:"/collections/digestion"},faqTitle:"Sensitive Stomachs in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"What should I feed a dog with a sensitive stomach?",a:"A consistent, easily digestible diet suits most sensitive dogs. Your vet can recommend a suitable option, and any changes should be made gradually."},{q:"Why does my dog get an upset tummy so easily?",a:"Some dogs are simply more sensitive. Consistent feeding, avoiding rich treats and supporting the gut usually make a big difference."},{q:"Do probiotics help sensitive stomachs?",a:"Yes, supporting healthy gut bacteria can help steady digestion in dogs prone to upsets, especially given consistently."}],related:["probiotics-for-dogs","dog-obesity-weight-management"]},"probiotics-for-dogs":{title:"Probiotics for Dogs: Do They Help?",cat:"Internal Health",catKey:"internal",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Probiotics have become a go-to for digestive health, in dogs as well as people. But what do they actually do, and when are they genuinely useful? Here\u2019s a clear, no-hype look at probiotics for dogs and how to use them well.",sections:[{h:"What probiotics do",html:"<p>Your dog\u2019s gut is home to billions of bacteria that help digest food and support overall health. Probiotics are beneficial live bacteria that help maintain or restore a healthy balance in the gut, particularly useful when that balance has been disrupted.</p>"},{h:"When they help most",html:"<p>Probiotics are most useful at times of digestive upset or change.</p><ul><li>After a bout of diarrhoea or an upset tummy</li><li>During or after a course of antibiotics</li><li>When changing diet or during stressful events</li><li>For dogs with ongoing sensitive digestion</li></ul>"},{h:"Using them well",html:"<p>A few simple tips get the best results.</p><ul><li>Choose a probiotic made specifically for dogs</li><li>Give consistently, benefits build over time</li><li>Pair with a settled, consistent diet</li><li>Use alongside, not instead of, veterinary advice for ongoing problems</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Loose stools","Sensitive tummy","After antibiotics","Diet change"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> If your dog has ongoing or severe digestive problems, see your vet rather than relying on probiotics alone, they can rule out underlying causes, with gut support playing a helpful supporting role.",shop:{h:"Shop gut & digestion support",href:"/collections/digestion"},faqTitle:"Probiotics for Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Do probiotics really work for dogs?",a:"They can genuinely help restore healthy gut bacteria, particularly after upsets, antibiotics or diet changes. They work best given consistently."},{q:"Can I give my dog human probiotics?",a:"It\u2019s best to use a probiotic formulated for dogs, as the strains and doses are tailored to them."},{q:"How long until I see a difference?",a:"Some dogs settle within days, but probiotics generally work best given consistently over a longer period."}],related:["sensitive-stomach-dogs","dog-obesity-weight-management"]},"dog-drinking-lots-of-water":{title:"Why Is My Dog Drinking So Much Water?",cat:"Internal Health",catKey:"internal",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Dogs drink more in hot weather or after exercise, that\u2019s normal. But a sustained, unexplained increase in thirst (and weeing) is one of the most important early warning signs in dog health, and it always deserves attention. Here\u2019s what could be behind it.",sections:[{h:"What counts as 'too much'?",html:"<p>As a rough guide, dogs drink around 50ml of water per kilo of body weight a day. A clear, ongoing increase beyond your dog\u2019s normal, especially with more frequent or larger wees, is worth noting. The medical term is polydipsia.</p>"},{h:"Possible causes",html:"<p>Increased thirst can signal several conditions, which is exactly why it shouldn\u2019t be ignored.</p><ul><li>Kidney disease</li><li>Diabetes</li><li>Hormonal conditions such as Cushing\u2019s disease</li><li>Certain medications</li><li>Sometimes simply hot weather, diet or activity</li></ul>"},{h:"What to do",html:"<p>Track it and get it checked.</p><ul><li>Note roughly how much your dog is drinking over a few days</li><li>Watch for other signs, weight loss, appetite changes, lethargy</li><li>Don\u2019t restrict water, which can be dangerous</li><li>Book a vet visit to find the cause</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Drinking more","Weeing more","Weight loss","Lethargy"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> A lasting increase in thirst always warrants a vet visit. A simple blood and urine test can identify the cause early, when conditions like kidney disease and diabetes are most manageable.",shop:{h:"Shop internal health support",href:"/collections/digestion"},faqTitle:"Why Is My Dog Drinking So Much Water?, answered",faq:[{q:"Should I limit my dog\u2019s water if they\u2019re drinking a lot?",a:"No, never restrict water, as this can be dangerous. Instead, get the underlying cause investigated by your vet."},{q:"How much water is normal?",a:"Roughly 50ml per kilo of body weight per day, but it varies with weather, diet and activity. A sustained change from your dog\u2019s normal is the key thing."},{q:"Is increased thirst serious?",a:"It can be an early sign of conditions like kidney disease or diabetes, which are far easier to manage when caught early, so it\u2019s always worth checking."}],related:["kidney-disease-in-dogs","senior-dog-care-guide"]},"dog-obesity-weight-management":{title:"Dog Obesity: Weight Management That Actually Works",cat:"Internal Health",catKey:"internal",meta:["8 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Excess weight is one of the most common, and most preventable, health problems in dogs. It strains joints, organs and the heart, and shortens lives. The good news is that even modest, gradual weight loss brings big benefits, and it\u2019s very achievable with a clear plan.",sections:[{h:"Why a healthy weight matters",html:"<p>Carrying extra weight puts strain on the joints (worsening arthritis), the heart and the organs, and reduces energy and quality of life. Studies show lean dogs live longer, healthier lives, so weight management is one of the kindest things you can do.</p>"},{h:"Is my dog overweight?",html:"<p>You don\u2019t need scales to get a good sense of it.</p><ul><li>You should be able to feel the ribs easily without pressing hard</li><li>A visible 'waist' when viewed from above</li><li>A tucked-up tummy when viewed from the side</li><li>Tiring quickly, reluctance to exercise, or struggling in the heat</li></ul>"},{h:"A plan that works",html:"<p>Slow and steady wins, crash diets aren\u2019t safe or sustainable.</p><ul><li>Measure meals accurately rather than guessing</li><li>Cut back on treats, or use part of the daily food as treats</li><li>Switch to a lower-calorie or weight-management topper</li><li>Increase gentle activity gradually</li><li>Weigh-ins every couple of weeks to track progress</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Weight gain","Hard to feel ribs","Low energy","Tires easily"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Ask your vet to confirm your dog\u2019s ideal weight and rule out medical causes of weight gain. They can set a safe target and rate of loss, and check progress along the way.",shop:{h:"Shop weight management support",href:"/collections/digestion"},faqTitle:"Dog Obesity, answered",faq:[{q:"How quickly should a dog lose weight?",a:"Slowly and steadily, usually around 1 to 2% of body weight per week. Rapid weight loss isn\u2019t safe, so check targets with your vet."},{q:"Can I still give treats?",a:"Yes, in moderation, use low-calorie options or set aside part of the daily food allowance for treats so you don\u2019t add extra."},{q:"Will losing weight help my dog\u2019s joints?",a:"Definitely, less weight means less strain on the joints, which often noticeably improves comfort and mobility."}],related:["dog-arthritis-signs-and-support","exercise-for-dogs-with-joint-problems","senior-dog-care-guide"]},"kidney-disease-in-dogs":{title:"Kidney Disease in Dogs: Support and Care",cat:"Internal Health",catKey:"internal",meta:["7 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"The kidneys filter waste and balance fluids, so when they don\u2019t work as well as they should, it affects the whole body. Kidney disease is more common in older dogs and needs a vet-led plan, but supportive care at home makes a real difference to comfort and quality of life.",sections:[{h:"Understanding kidney disease",html:"<p>Kidney disease can come on suddenly (acute) or develop slowly over months and years (chronic). Chronic kidney disease is common in senior dogs, where kidney function gradually declines. It can\u2019t usually be reversed, but it can often be managed well for a long time with the right care.</p>"},{h:"Signs to look for",html:"<p>Increased thirst and urination are classic early signs.</p><ul><li>Drinking noticeably more than usual</li><li>Weeing more often or in larger amounts</li><li>Weight loss and reduced appetite</li><li>Lethargy, a dull coat or bad breath</li></ul>"},{h:"Supporting kidney health",html:"<p>Management is led by your vet, but day-to-day care matters too.</p><ul><li>Follow any vet-recommended kidney diet closely</li><li>Always have fresh water available</li><li>Kidney-support supplements alongside a vet-led plan</li><li>Keep stress low and routines consistent</li><li>Attend regular check-ups and blood tests</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Drinking more","Weeing more","Weight loss","Low appetite"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Increased thirst and urination always deserve a vet visit. Early diagnosis of kidney disease means it can be managed sooner, which makes a big difference to how well and how long your dog does.",shop:{h:"Shop internal & kidney support",href:"/collections/digestion"},faqTitle:"Kidney Disease in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Is kidney disease in dogs curable?",a:"Acute cases can sometimes recover with prompt treatment. Chronic kidney disease isn\u2019t usually reversible, but it can often be managed well for a long time."},{q:"Do kidney supplements help?",a:"They can support kidney health as part of a vet-led plan, alongside the right diet and regular monitoring, not instead of them."},{q:"Why is my dog suddenly drinking so much?",a:"Increased thirst can signal kidney disease, diabetes or other conditions, so it always warrants a vet check."}],related:["dog-drinking-lots-of-water","senior-dog-care-guide"]},"grass-seeds-and-dogs-paws":{title:"Grass Seeds and Your Dog\u2019s Paws: A Summer Hazard",cat:"Paws & Limbs",catKey:"paws",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"In summer, those little arrow-shaped grass seeds become a surprisingly serious hazard for dogs. They\u2019re designed to burrow, and they can work their way into paws, ears and skin, causing pain and infection. Here\u2019s how to keep your dog safe.",sections:[{h:"Why grass seeds are a problem",html:"<p>Grass seeds have a sharp tip and backward-facing barbs, so once they catch on fur they only travel one way, inwards. The most common spot is between the toes, where they can pierce the skin and cause a painful, swollen abscess. They can also lodge in ears, eyes and skin.</p>"},{h:"Signs a grass seed is causing trouble",html:"<p>Catching it early prevents bigger problems.</p><ul><li>Sudden, intense licking or chewing of one paw</li><li>A swollen, red lump between the toes, sometimes weeping</li><li>Head shaking or pawing at an ear</li><li>Squinting or a runny eye</li></ul>"},{h:"Prevention and what to do",html:"<p>Checking and trimming go a long way.</p><ul><li>Check paws, ears and coat after walks in long, seedy grass</li><li>Keep the fur between the toes and around the ears trimmed</li><li>Avoid walking through tall, seeding grass in summer</li><li>Remove any seeds you can see caught in the coat straightaway</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Sudden paw licking","Swollen lump","Head shaking","Squinting eye"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet promptly if you suspect a grass seed has gone into the skin, ear or eye, they often need removing professionally before they cause an abscess or travel further.",shop:{h:"Shop paw & skin care",href:"/collections/skin-coat"},faqTitle:"Grass Seeds and Your Dog\u2019s Paws, answered",faq:[{q:"Where do grass seeds usually get stuck?",a:"Most often between the toes, but also in the ears, eyes, nose and skin folds. Anywhere fur can catch them."},{q:"How do I prevent grass seed problems?",a:"Check and groom after walks, keep paw and ear fur trimmed, and avoid long seeding grass in summer."},{q:"Can I remove a grass seed myself?",a:"Only if it\u2019s clearly caught in the fur and hasn\u2019t pierced the skin. Once it\u2019s under the skin or in an ear or eye, it\u2019s a job for the vet."}],related:["cracked-paw-pads-dogs","dog-licking-paws","ear-infections-in-dogs"]},"cracked-paw-pads-dogs":{title:"Cracked Paw Pads in Dogs: Care and Prevention",cat:"Paws & Limbs",catKey:"paws",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Your dog\u2019s paw pads take a daily battering, hot pavements, icy ground, rough terrain and harsh chemicals all take their toll. Dry, cracked pads are sore and can let in infection. A little care keeps them soft, healthy and resilient.",sections:[{h:"Why pads crack",html:"<p>Pads can dry out and crack from hot or cold surfaces, walking on rough ground, contact with salt or chemicals, or simply age. Some dogs are more prone than others, and very dry pads can split and become painful or infected.</p>"},{h:"Signs to look for",html:"<p>Check the pads regularly, especially after tough walks.</p><ul><li>Dry, rough or flaky pads</li><li>Visible cracks or splits</li><li>Limping or licking at the paws</li><li>Redness or soreness in the cracks</li></ul>"},{h:"Care and prevention",html:"<p>Moisturise, protect and avoid the worst surfaces.</p><ul><li>Apply a soothing paw balm to soften and protect pads</li><li>Rinse and dry paws after walks on salted or gritted roads</li><li>Avoid hot pavements in summer, walk early or late</li><li>Keep the fur between pads trimmed</li><li>Build up tough terrain gradually rather than all at once</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Dry pads","Cracks or splits","Limping","Licking paws"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if cracks are deep, bleeding, or look infected, or if your dog is lame, sore pads sometimes need treatment as well as good home care.",shop:{h:"Shop paw balms & care",href:"/collections/skin-coat"},faqTitle:"Cracked Paw Pads in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"How do I soften my dog\u2019s cracked pads?",a:"A dog-safe paw balm applied regularly softens and protects the pads. Avoid human products, which aren\u2019t designed for dogs."},{q:"Can hot pavements hurt my dog\u2019s pads?",a:"Yes, hot surfaces can burn pads. As a rule, if it\u2019s too hot for the back of your hand for seven seconds, it\u2019s too hot for paws."},{q:"Should I use paw balm in winter too?",a:"Yes, cold, salted and gritted surfaces dry pads out, so balm and rinsing paws after walks both help."}],related:["dog-licking-paws","grass-seeds-and-dogs-paws","itchy-skin-allergies-in-dogs"]},"dog-licking-paws":{title:"Why Is My Dog Licking Their Paws?",cat:"Paws & Limbs",catKey:"paws",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"A bit of paw-licking is normal grooming. But if your dog is constantly licking, chewing or nibbling their paws, sometimes to the point of red, sore skin, there\u2019s usually an underlying reason worth tracking down. Here are the common causes and what helps.",sections:[{h:"Common reasons for paw-licking",html:"<p>Allergies are the number-one cause, to pollen, grass, food or things in the environment. Other reasons include irritation, a trapped grass seed, dry or cracked pads, a paw injury, or even boredom and stress when it becomes a habit.</p>"},{h:"What to check",html:"<p>Have a good look (and sniff) at the paws.</p><ul><li>Redness or soreness between the toes and on the pads</li><li>A brown stain on the fur from constant licking</li><li>Cracked or dry pads</li><li>Anything stuck between the toes, like a grass seed</li><li>A pattern, after walks, certain times of year, or when bored</li></ul>"},{h:"How to help",html:"<p>Soothe the skin and tackle the cause.</p><ul><li>Wipe and dry paws after walks, especially in allergy season</li><li>Soothing balms for dry or irritated pads</li><li>Omega oils to support skin and coat from within</li><li>Keep on top of flea and parasite prevention</li><li>Address boredom with enrichment if licking is habitual</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Constant licking","Red between toes","Stained fur","Cracked pads"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if the skin is broken, sore, swollen or smelly, or the licking is constant, persistent paw-licking often needs an allergy or infection treating before it will settle.",shop:{h:"Shop paw & skin care",href:"/collections/skin-coat"},faqTitle:"Why Is My Dog Licking Their Paws?, answered",faq:[{q:"Why does my dog lick their paws after walks?",a:"It\u2019s often a sign of contact allergies to grass or pollen. Wiping the paws after walks and a soothing routine can help."},{q:"Can paw-licking be a habit?",a:"Yes, what starts with irritation can become a habit, especially in bored or anxious dogs. Enrichment and addressing the cause both help."},{q:"Do omega oils help?",a:"Omega oils support the skin barrier and coat condition, which can reduce dryness and irritation over time."}],related:["itchy-skin-allergies-in-dogs","cracked-paw-pads-dogs","seasonal-allergies-in-dogs"]},"hock-achilles-injuries-dogs":{title:"Hock and Achilles Injuries in Dogs",cat:"Paws & Limbs",catKey:"paws",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"The hock is the ankle joint of your dog\u2019s back leg, and the Achilles tendon above it does a huge amount of work every step. Strains and injuries here cause lameness and a dropped, flat-footed stance. Here\u2019s how to recognise the problem and support recovery.",sections:[{h:"Understanding hock and Achilles injuries",html:"<p>The Achilles tendon connects the calf muscles to the heel and powers the push-off in every stride. Injuries range from strains to partial or full tears, and can cause the hock to drop towards the ground. The hock joint itself can also be injured or affected by arthritis.</p>"},{h:"Signs to look for",html:"<p>Watch the back-leg stance and how your dog moves.</p><ul><li>A dropped hock, the ankle sitting low to the ground</li><li>Limping or reluctance to bear weight on a back leg</li><li>Swelling around the hock or Achilles</li><li>Stiffness, especially after rest</li></ul>"},{h:"Supporting recovery",html:"<p>Rest and support are key while the injury heals.</p><ul><li>Rest and restricted activity as advised by your vet</li><li>A hock or tarsal support to stabilise the joint</li><li>Non-slip flooring to prevent slips and twists</li><li>Gradual, controlled return to exercise</li><li>Keep your dog lean to reduce strain</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Dropped hock","Limping","Swelling","Stiff after rest"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet for any back-leg lameness or a dropped hock, especially after exercise or a fall, Achilles injuries in particular need prompt assessment to give the best outcome.",shop:{h:"Shop hock & limb support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Hock and Achilles Injuries in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"What is a dropped hock?",a:"It\u2019s when the ankle joint sits lower than normal, often because the Achilles tendon or supporting structures are injured or weakened."},{q:"Can a support brace help?",a:"A hock support can stabilise the joint and aid comfort during recovery, alongside rest and your vet\u2019s treatment plan."},{q:"How long does recovery take?",a:"It varies with the injury, from a few weeks for a mild strain to longer for tendon damage. Follow your vet\u2019s timeline."}],related:["carpal-hyperextension-in-dogs","cruciate-ligament-injury-dogs","rear-leg-weakness-in-dogs"]},"carpal-hyperextension-in-dogs":{title:"Carpal Hyperextension in Dogs",cat:"Paws & Limbs",catKey:"paws",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Carpal hyperextension is when the wrist (carpus) joints drop closer to the ground than they should, giving a flat-footed, dropped-wrist stance. It can follow an injury or develop over time, and supportive care helps affected dogs stay comfortable.",sections:[{h:"What is carpal hyperextension?",html:"<p>The carpus is the wrist joint of the front leg. When the ligaments that support it weaken or are injured, the joint over-extends and drops, so the dog ends up bearing weight lower down the leg than normal. This changes their stance and can be uncomfortable.</p>"},{h:"Signs to look for",html:"<p>The change in the front legs is usually visible.</p><ul><li>A dropped, flat-footed front-leg stance</li><li>Wrists sitting lower to the ground than normal</li><li>Front-leg lameness or stiffness</li><li>Reluctance to exercise</li></ul>"},{h:"Supporting the wrist",html:"<p>A carpal support and sensible management protect the joint.</p><ul><li>A carpal support wrap or brace to stabilise the wrist</li><li>Soft, supportive bedding and non-slip floors</li><li>Keeping your dog lean to reduce load</li><li>Controlled, low-impact exercise as advised by your vet</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Dropped wrists","Flat-footed stance","Front-leg limp","Stiffness"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet to assess the cause and severity. Some cases follow an injury and may need specific treatment, while others are managed well with a supportive wrap and sensible care.",shop:{h:"Shop carpal & limb support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Carpal Hyperextension in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"What causes carpal hyperextension?",a:"It can follow a fall or injury that damages the supporting ligaments, or develop gradually. Your vet can determine the cause."},{q:"Can a wrist support help?",a:"Yes, a carpal support wrap stabilises the joint and aids comfort, particularly during management and recovery."},{q:"Will my dog still be able to walk?",a:"Most affected dogs stay mobile with the right support and sensible exercise, though the approach depends on the cause and severity."}],related:["knuckling-in-dogs","hock-achilles-injuries-dogs","dog-arthritis-signs-and-support"]},"knuckling-in-dogs":{title:"Knuckling in Dogs: What It Means and How to Help",cat:"Paws & Limbs",catKey:"paws",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Knuckling is when a dog walks on the top of their paw instead of the pad, or the paw drags and scuffs along the ground. It often points to a problem with the nerves or mobility, so it\u2019s worth a vet check, but there\u2019s also plenty you can do to help.",sections:[{h:"What causes knuckling?",html:"<p>Knuckling happens when the signals telling the paw to position itself correctly aren\u2019t getting through properly, or the leg is too weak to lift the paw fully. Causes range from nerve and spinal conditions to arthritis, injury and general weakness, especially in older dogs.</p>"},{h:"Signs to look for",html:"<p>It can be subtle at first.</p><ul><li>The paw turning under or dragging along the ground</li><li>Scuffed, worn nails on the affected foot</li><li>Grazes or sores on the top of the paw</li><li>Stumbling or an uneven, scuffing gait</li></ul>"},{h:"How to help",html:"<p>Protecting the paw and supporting the leg are the priorities while the cause is investigated.</p><ul><li>Anti-knuckling boots or socks to protect dragging paws</li><li>A support harness to assist movement</li><li>Non-slip flooring to give better grip</li><li>Gentle, regular exercise and physiotherapy to maintain strength</li><li>Keep nails trimmed and check paws daily for grazes</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Dragging paws","Scuffed nails","Stumbling","Grazes on paw"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Have knuckling checked by your vet, especially if it comes on suddenly or affects the back legs, it can be a sign of a treatable nerve or spinal condition that benefits from early attention.",shop:{h:"Shop paw protection & support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Knuckling in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Is knuckling an emergency?",a:"Sudden knuckling, particularly with weakness, should be checked promptly. Gradual knuckling in an older dog still deserves a vet visit to find the cause."},{q:"Do anti-knuckling boots help?",a:"Yes, they protect the paw from grazing and dragging and can help reposition the foot, improving comfort and confidence."},{q:"Can knuckling be reversed?",a:"It depends on the cause. Some causes are very treatable; others are managed with support. A diagnosis is the first step."}],related:["degenerative-myelopathy-in-dogs","rear-leg-weakness-in-dogs","nerve-weakness-in-dogs"]},"orthopaedic-dog-beds-guide":{title:"Orthopaedic Dog Beds: Why They Matter for Joint Health",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Your dog spends a huge part of their day lying down, so the bed they rest on matters more than you might think, especially for dogs with sore joints. A supportive orthopaedic bed cushions pressure points and helps them get up more easily. Here\u2019s what to look for.",sections:[{h:"Why the right bed matters",html:"<p>Thin or saggy beds let a dog\u2019s weight press their joints against the hard floor, which is uncomfortable for arthritic or older dogs. A firm, supportive bed distributes weight evenly, relieves pressure on hips, elbows and shoulders, and makes getting up easier.</p>"},{h:"What to look for",html:"<p>Not all 'orthopaedic' beds are equal.</p><ul><li>Genuine memory or high-density foam that doesn\u2019t bottom out</li><li>A firm, supportive base rather than soft stuffing</li><li>Low or easy access for dogs who struggle to step in</li><li>A washable, durable cover</li><li>The right size, big enough to stretch out fully</li></ul>"},{h:"Getting the most from it",html:"<p>Placement and routine help too.</p><ul><li>Put it in a warm, quiet, draught-free spot</li><li>Keep it away from cold, hard floors</li><li>Add a familiar blanket so it smells like home</li><li>Have one upstairs and down if stairs are difficult</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Restless sleep","Hard to get up","Stiff joints","Older dog"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> If your dog seems uncomfortable however they lie, struggles to settle, or is increasingly stiff, mention it to your vet, a supportive bed helps, but pain may also need treating.",shop:{h:"Shop orthopaedic beds & comfort",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Orthopaedic Dog Beds, answered",faq:[{q:"Do orthopaedic beds really help arthritic dogs?",a:"Yes, proper support cushions sore joints and pressure points, helps dogs rest better and makes rising easier."},{q:"What size bed should I get?",a:"Big enough for your dog to lie fully stretched out. Measure them lying down and add a margin."},{q:"Is memory foam best?",a:"High-density or memory foam that holds its shape gives the best support. Avoid thin pads or soft stuffing that flattens."}],related:["dog-arthritis-signs-and-support","senior-dog-care-guide","dog-struggling-to-get-up"]},"exercise-for-dogs-with-joint-problems":{title:"The Right Exercise for Dogs with Joint Problems",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["7 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"When a dog has joint problems, it\u2019s tempting to wrap them in cotton wool. But the right exercise keeps muscles strong, joints mobile and weight in check. The trick is choosing low-impact, consistent activity over high-impact bursts. Here\u2019s how to get the balance right.",sections:[{h:"Why exercise matters",html:"<p>Movement keeps the muscles that support the joints strong, helps maintain a healthy weight, and keeps the joints themselves mobile. A dog that stops moving loses muscle quickly, which makes joint problems worse, so the goal is the right amount, not none.</p>"},{h:"The best types of exercise",html:"<p>Low-impact, steady and predictable is the rule.</p><ul><li>Lead walks on soft, even ground</li><li>Swimming and hydrotherapy, superb for joints</li><li>Gentle, controlled sniffing walks to keep the mind busy too</li><li>Short, frequent sessions rather than one long outing</li></ul>"},{h:"What to avoid",html:"<p>High-impact activity puts sudden, jarring load through sore joints.</p><ul><li>Repetitive ball or frisbee chasing with sharp turns</li><li>Jumping on and off furniture or in and out of the car</li><li>Rough play and sudden sprints</li><li>Long, exhausting walks that leave your dog stiff the next day</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Stiff after exercise","Tires quickly","Slows on walks","Reluctant to move"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> If your dog is newly diagnosed or in pain, ask your vet or a canine physiotherapist to tailor an exercise plan. They can advise the right amount and build it up safely.",shop:{h:"Shop mobility & rehab support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"The Right Exercise for Dogs with Joint Problems, answered",faq:[{q:"How much should a dog with arthritis walk?",a:"Little and often is the rule, several short, gentle walks usually beat one long one. Your vet can tailor it to your dog."},{q:"Is swimming good for dogs with joint problems?",a:"Yes, it builds strength and fitness with very little strain on the joints, which is why hydrotherapy is so widely recommended."},{q:"Should I stop exercising my stiff dog?",a:"No, gentle, regular exercise is important. Just choose low-impact activity and avoid high-impact bursts."}],related:["dog-arthritis-signs-and-support","hydrotherapy-swimming-for-dogs","stiff-joints-in-dogs"]},"dog-struggling-to-get-up":{title:"Helping a Dog That Struggles to Get Up",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Watching your dog struggle to get up, paddling on a slippery floor, or needing a few attempts to stand, is hard to see. It usually points to joint pain, weakness or both. The good news is that a few practical changes can restore their confidence and comfort.",sections:[{h:"Why it happens",html:"<p>Difficulty rising is most often caused by arthritis, hip or joint pain, or muscle weakness, frequently a combination, especially in older dogs. Slippery floors make everything harder, because dogs can\u2019t get the grip they need to push up.</p>"},{h:"What to look for",html:"<p>Notice when and how the struggle happens.</p><ul><li>Several attempts to stand, especially after rest</li><li>Paddling or slipping on smooth floors</li><li>Stiffness that eases once they\u2019re up and moving</li><li>Reluctance to lie down or get comfortable</li></ul>"},{h:"How to help",html:"<p>Think traction, support and comfort.</p><ul><li>Add grip, rugs and runners over hard floors are transformative</li><li>A support harness to gently assist them up</li><li>A firm, supportive orthopaedic bed (not too low)</li><li>Keep your dog lean and their muscles gently active</li><li>Joint support and warmth, especially in winter</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Several attempts to rise","Slipping","Stiff after rest","Paddling"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if your dog suddenly can\u2019t get up, seems in pain, or is getting weaker, this needs investigating to find and treat the cause.",shop:{h:"Shop support harnesses & beds",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Helping a Dog That Struggles to Get Up, answered",faq:[{q:"Why does my old dog struggle to stand?",a:"Usually a mix of arthritis and muscle loss, often made worse by slippery floors. Grip, support and joint care help a lot."},{q:"Will a harness help?",a:"Yes, a support harness lets you gently take some weight and assist your dog up without straining them or yourself."},{q:"Are slippery floors really a problem?",a:"Very much so. Adding rugs and runners gives dogs the grip they need and prevents painful slips and failed attempts to rise."}],related:["dog-arthritis-signs-and-support","rear-leg-weakness-in-dogs","senior-dog-care-guide"]},"stiff-joints-in-dogs":{title:"Stiff Joints in Dogs: Why It Happens and How to Help",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"If your dog is stiff getting up, slow to warm up on walks, or hesitant on the stairs, their joints may be telling you something. Stiffness is one of the most common, and most treatable, early signs of joint problems. Here\u2019s how to ease it.",sections:[{h:"Why dogs get stiff joints",html:"<p>Stiffness usually comes down to inflammation and wear in the joints, most often from arthritis, but also from injuries, hip and elbow problems, or simply muscle loss with age. Cold weather and hard, slippery floors make it worse.</p>"},{h:"When to take notice",html:"<p>A dog that\u2019s stiff first thing but loosens up as they move is showing a classic early sign.</p><ul><li>Stiff or slow getting up, especially after rest</li><li>Taking a while to 'warm up' at the start of a walk</li><li>Reluctance to jump, climb or use stairs</li><li>Stiffness that\u2019s worse in cold or damp weather</li></ul>"},{h:"Easing stiff joints",html:"<p>The aim is to reduce strain, keep joints moving and support them day to day.</p><ul><li>Keep your dog lean, less weight, less strain</li><li>Gentle, regular exercise rather than big bursts</li><li>Warmth and a supportive orthopaedic bed</li><li>Grippy flooring and ramps to protect joints</li><li>Joint supplements as part of a long-term routine</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Stiff after rest","Slow to warm up","Reluctant to jump","Worse in cold"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if stiffness is persistent, worsening, or your dog seems in pain, they can pinpoint the cause and discuss pain relief that supportive products work alongside.",shop:{h:"Shop joint & mobility support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Stiff Joints in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Is stiffness always arthritis?",a:"Often, but not always, injuries, hip or elbow problems and muscle loss can all cause it. A vet check pinpoints the cause."},{q:"Do supplements help stiff joints?",a:"Joint supplements support the joints over time as part of a wider plan that includes weight control and sensible exercise."},{q:"Should I walk a stiff dog?",a:"Usually yes, gentle, regular movement helps. Little and often is better than occasional long walks."}],related:["dog-arthritis-signs-and-support","dog-struggling-to-get-up","exercise-for-dogs-with-joint-problems"]},"spondylosis-in-dogs":{title:"Spondylosis in Dogs: Stiff Spines in Older Dogs",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Spondylosis deformans is a degenerative change where bony spurs form along the spine. It\u2019s very common in older dogs and often causes no symptoms at all, but in some dogs it leads to stiffness and reduced flexibility. Here\u2019s how to recognise it and help.",sections:[{h:"What is spondylosis?",html:"<p>Over time, bony bridges (osteophytes) can form between the vertebrae of the spine, usually as a response to ageing and wear. In many dogs they\u2019re an incidental finding on an x-ray. In others, they reduce the spine\u2019s flexibility and cause stiffness or discomfort.</p>"},{h:"Signs it might be bothering your dog",html:"<p>Because it develops slowly, signs are easy to put down to ageing.</p><ul><li>Stiffness, especially after rest or in cold weather</li><li>Reduced flexibility, turning or twisting less easily</li><li>Reluctance to jump or climb</li><li>A slightly stiff or 'roached' posture</li></ul>"},{h:"Keeping the spine supple",html:"<p>Gentle, regular movement and good support are the cornerstones of management.</p><ul><li>Little-and-often exercise to keep the back mobile</li><li>A supportive orthopaedic bed and warm rest areas</li><li>Ramps to avoid jarring the spine jumping on and off furniture</li><li>Keep your dog lean to reduce strain</li><li>Joint and mobility support as part of a routine</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Stiffness","Less flexible","Reluctant to jump","Stiff posture"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if your dog seems painful, suddenly stiffer, or develops any weakness or wobbliness in the back legs, as this needs investigating to rule out other spinal conditions.",shop:{h:"Shop spine & mobility support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Spondylosis in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Is spondylosis painful?",a:"Often it isn\u2019t and causes no problems. In some dogs it leads to stiffness or discomfort that benefits from support and gentle exercise."},{q:"Can my dog still exercise?",a:"Yes, gentle, regular exercise is good for keeping the spine mobile. Avoid high-impact jumping and twisting."},{q:"Does it get worse?",a:"It can progress slowly with age, but many dogs stay comfortable for years with sensible management."}],related:["dog-arthritis-signs-and-support","senior-dog-care-guide","ivdd-in-dogs"]},"elbow-dysplasia-in-dogs":{title:"Elbow Dysplasia in Dogs: Signs and Support",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Elbow dysplasia describes several developmental problems in the elbow joint that lead to a poor fit, inflammation and, over time, arthritis. It\u2019s a common cause of front-leg lameness, particularly in growing large-breed dogs. With early support, affected dogs can stay comfortable and active.",sections:[{h:"Understanding elbow dysplasia",html:"<p>The elbow is a complex joint made of three bones that need to grow together perfectly. When they don\u2019t, the joint surfaces don\u2019t fit smoothly, causing rubbing, inflammation and secondary arthritis. It\u2019s largely genetic and often appears as a young dog grows.</p>"},{h:"Common signs",html:"<p>Front-leg lameness that comes and goes is the classic picture.</p><ul><li>Limping or stiffness in a front leg, often worse after exercise</li><li>Reluctance to exercise or play</li><li>Holding the elbow slightly out from the body</li><li>Swelling or reduced range of movement in the elbow</li></ul>"},{h:"Managing it at home",html:"<p>Alongside any veterinary treatment, day-to-day support protects the joint and keeps your dog comfortable.</p><ul><li>Keep your dog lean to reduce load on the elbow</li><li>An elbow brace or support for added stability and comfort</li><li>Controlled, low-impact exercise rather than high-impact play</li><li>Soft, supportive bedding and warm rest areas</li><li>Joint support as part of a long-term plan</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Front-leg limp","Stiffness","Elbow held out","Swelling"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet for any persistent front-leg lameness, especially in a growing large-breed dog. Early diagnosis means more options and better long-term comfort.",shop:{h:"Shop elbow & joint support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Elbow Dysplasia in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Which breeds are prone to elbow dysplasia?",a:"Large and giant breeds such as Labradors, German Shepherds, Rottweilers and Bernese Mountain Dogs are most at risk."},{q:"Is it the same as arthritis?",a:"No, but elbow dysplasia commonly leads to arthritis in the joint over time, which is why early support matters."},{q:"Can an elbow brace help?",a:"A supportive brace can aid comfort and stability, particularly during management and recovery, alongside weight control and sensible exercise."}],related:["dog-arthritis-signs-and-support","stiff-joints-in-dogs"]},"luxating-patella-in-dogs":{title:"Luxating Patella in Dogs: The Skipping Step Explained",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"If your dog occasionally skips a step, holds a back leg up for a stride or two, then carries on as normal, a luxating patella could be behind it. It\u2019s a common knee problem, especially in smaller breeds, and most dogs manage really well with the right care.",sections:[{h:"What is a luxating patella?",html:"<p>The patella is the kneecap, which normally glides in a groove at the front of the knee. With a luxating patella, it slips out of that groove, usually to the inside, causing a brief skip or hop until it pops back. Over time the repeated movement can irritate the joint and contribute to arthritis.</p>"},{h:"Signs to look for",html:"<p>It\u2019s often intermittent, which is why owners sometimes miss it for a while.</p><ul><li>An occasional skip or hop while walking or running</li><li>Briefly holding a back leg up, then using it normally</li><li>Sitting with a leg held out to the side</li><li>Stiffness after rest in more advanced cases</li></ul>"},{h:"Helping your dog at home",html:"<p>Mild cases are often managed conservatively, with surgery reserved for more severe grades. Supportive care helps comfort and protects the joint either way.</p><ul><li>Keep your dog lean to reduce strain on the knee</li><li>A supportive knee brace where appropriate</li><li>Non-slip flooring and ramps to avoid awkward twists</li><li>Low-impact exercise over jumping and sudden turns</li><li>Joint support as part of a long-term plan</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Skipping steps","Hopping briefly","Leg held out","Stiff after rest"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if the skipping becomes frequent, your dog is lame or in pain, or the leg seems to be getting worse. They can grade the knee and advise whether management or surgery is best.",shop:{h:"Shop knee & joint support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Luxating Patella in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Which dogs get luxating patella?",a:"It\u2019s most common in small and toy breeds, but larger dogs can be affected too, sometimes after an injury."},{q:"Does it always need surgery?",a:"No. Many mild cases are managed well with weight control, sensible exercise and joint support. Higher grades may need surgery."},{q:"Can a brace help?",a:"A supportive knee brace can aid stability and comfort, particularly during conservative management or recovery."}],related:["cruciate-ligament-injury-dogs","dog-arthritis-signs-and-support","exercise-for-dogs-with-joint-problems"]},"senior-dog-care-guide":{title:"Senior Dog Care: Keeping Older Dogs Mobile and Comfortable",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["9 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Watching your dog grow older is bittersweet, but the senior years can be some of the most rewarding. With a few thoughtful adjustments to their routine and environment, you can keep an older dog comfortable, mobile and content for years to come. Here\u2019s how.",sections:[{h:"How dogs change as they age",html:"<p>Just like us, dogs slow down with age. Joints stiffen, muscles soften, senses dull and sleep increases. They may be less tolerant of cold, take longer to recover from activity, and need a gentler pace. None of this means they can\u2019t enjoy a great quality of life, it just means their needs change.</p>"},{h:"Signs your dog is feeling their age",html:"<p>Spotting changes early lets you stay ahead of them.</p><ul><li>Stiffness, slowing down or reluctance to jump and climb</li><li>Sleeping more and tiring sooner</li><li>Cloudy eyes, reduced hearing or bumping into things</li><li>Weight changes, up or down</li><li>Greying muzzle and a generally gentler pace</li></ul>"},{h:"Keeping a senior dog comfortable",html:"<p>Small, consistent changes add up to a big difference in an older dog\u2019s comfort and confidence.</p><ul><li>A supportive orthopaedic bed in a warm, draught-free spot</li><li>Non-slip flooring and ramps to protect ageing joints</li><li>Shorter, more frequent walks to keep them moving without overdoing it</li><li>Joint support and, where needed, a support harness</li><li>Keep them lean, keep teeth healthy, and keep up regular vet check-ups</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Stiffness","Sleeping more","Slowing down","Tires easily"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Senior dogs benefit from regular vet check-ups, ideally twice a year, so changes are caught early. Always get sudden changes in mobility, appetite, drinking or behaviour checked, as these can signal treatable conditions.",shop:{h:"Shop senior mobility & comfort",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Senior Dog Care, answered",faq:[{q:"When is a dog considered senior?",a:"It varies by size, larger breeds age faster, often counting as senior from around 6 to 7, while small breeds may not until 9 to 10."},{q:"How can I keep my old dog mobile?",a:"Keep them lean, walk little and often, add grip to floors, use ramps and a harness where needed, and support their joints. Gentle, regular movement is key."},{q:"My senior dog is sleeping a lot, is that normal?",a:"More sleep is normal with age, but a sudden increase, or other changes alongside it, is worth a vet check to rule out anything treatable."}],related:["dog-arthritis-signs-and-support","rear-leg-weakness-in-dogs","dental-disease-in-dogs"]},"rear-leg-weakness-in-dogs":{title:"Rear Leg Weakness in Dogs: Causes and How to Help",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Noticing your dog\u2019s back legs going wobbly, weak or giving way is understandably worrying. Rear leg weakness isn\u2019t a single condition but a sign that can point to several causes, from arthritis to nerve problems. Here\u2019s how to think about it and help your dog stay on their feet.",sections:[{h:"Common causes of rear leg weakness",html:"<p>Because the back legs rely on healthy joints, muscles, nerves and a healthy spine, weakness can come from any of these. The most common causes are arthritis and hip problems, spinal conditions like IVDD, nerve conditions such as degenerative myelopathy, and simple muscle loss with age.</p>"},{h:"What to look out for",html:"<p>The way the weakness shows up gives useful clues, so it\u2019s worth noting the detail for your vet.</p><ul><li>A wobbly, swaying or unsteady back end</li><li>Difficulty getting up, especially after rest</li><li>Dragging or scuffing the back paws</li><li>Tiring quickly or sitting down on walks</li><li>Slipping on smooth floors</li></ul>"},{h:"Helping a dog with weak back legs",html:"<p>Whatever the cause, the principles are similar: support the back end, protect against slips and falls, and keep the muscles as strong as possible.</p><ul><li>A rear support harness for stairs, getting up and walks</li><li>Non-slip rugs and runners over hard floors</li><li>Ramps for the car, sofa and steps</li><li>Gentle, regular exercise to maintain muscle, little and often</li><li>A supportive bed and a warm, draught-free place to rest</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Wobbly back end","Dragging","Struggling to rise","Tires quickly"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Always have new or worsening rear leg weakness checked by your vet, especially if it comes on suddenly. Identifying the cause means your dog gets the right treatment, and you can choose the aids that help most.",shop:{h:"Shop support harnesses & aids",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Rear Leg Weakness in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Why are my old dog\u2019s back legs getting weak?",a:"In senior dogs it\u2019s often a mix of arthritis and natural muscle loss, sometimes with a nerve component. A vet check pinpoints the cause so you can support it properly."},{q:"Can a harness help weak back legs?",a:"Yes, a rear support harness lets you gently take some weight and assist with stairs, getting up and walks, helping your dog stay active and confident."},{q:"Should I still exercise a dog with weak back legs?",a:"Usually yes, but gently and regularly rather than in big bursts. Your vet or a physiotherapist can advise the right amount for your dog."}],related:["degenerative-myelopathy-in-dogs","senior-dog-care-guide"]},"degenerative-myelopathy-in-dogs":{title:"Degenerative Myelopathy (CDRM) in Dogs: Support and Mobility Aids",cat:"Neurological",catKey:"neuro",meta:["7 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Degenerative myelopathy (DM, sometimes called CDRM) is a progressive condition of the spinal cord, most often seen in older, larger-breed dogs. It gradually affects the back legs. While there\u2019s no cure, the right support can keep dogs comfortable, mobile and happy for as long as possible.",sections:[{h:"What is degenerative myelopathy?",html:"<p>DM is a slow degeneration of the nerves in the spinal cord that carry signals to the back legs. Crucially, it\u2019s not painful, but it does cause increasing weakness and loss of coordination over time. It usually starts subtly and progresses gradually.</p>"},{h:"Recognising the signs",html:"<p>Early signs are easy to miss and can look like general ageing or arthritis, so they\u2019re worth a vet check.</p><ul><li>Wobbliness or swaying in the back end</li><li>Scuffing or knuckling, the paws dragging or turning under</li><li>Worn nails on the back feet</li><li>Difficulty with stairs, jumping or slippery floors</li><li>Gradually reducing strength in the hind legs</li></ul>"},{h:"Supporting a dog with DM",html:"<p>Management is all about maintaining mobility, dignity and quality of life. Physiotherapy and hydrotherapy can help keep muscles working, and a range of aids makes daily life easier.</p><ul><li>A rear support harness to assist walking and getting up</li><li>Anti-knuckling boots or socks to protect dragging paws</li><li>Ramps and non-slip flooring around the home</li><li>Gentle, regular exercise and physiotherapy to maintain muscle</li><li>Soft bedding and help staying clean and comfortable</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Knuckling","Rear weakness","Wobbliness","Worn nails"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet to confirm the diagnosis and rule out other, treatable causes of hind-leg weakness such as arthritis or IVDD. They can also guide you on physiotherapy, hydrotherapy and the mobility aids that will help most.",shop:{h:"Shop mobility aids & support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Degenerative Myelopathy (CDRM) in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Is degenerative myelopathy painful?",a:"DM itself isn\u2019t thought to be painful, but affected dogs often have other conditions like arthritis that are, so comfort and pain management still matter."},{q:"How fast does it progress?",a:"It varies between dogs, typically progressing over many months. Physiotherapy, staying active and supportive aids can help maintain quality of life along the way."},{q:"What aids help most?",a:"A rear support harness, anti-knuckling boots, ramps and non-slip flooring make the biggest day-to-day difference, alongside hydrotherapy and physio."}],related:["rear-leg-weakness-in-dogs","senior-dog-care-guide","ivdd-in-dogs"]},"post-surgery-recovery-for-dogs":{title:"Post-Surgery Recovery for Dogs: A Practical Care Guide",cat:"Essential Care",catKey:"essential",meta:["7 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Whether it\u2019s a routine spay or a bigger orthopaedic operation, the recovery period is where your care makes the biggest difference. A calm, comfortable dog that leaves their wound alone heals faster, here\u2019s how to make that happen.",sections:[{h:"The keys to a smooth recovery",html:"<p>Every operation is different, so your vet\u2019s aftercare instructions always come first. On top of those, three things matter most: protecting the surgical site, keeping your dog calm and rested, and watching for any signs of trouble.</p>"},{h:"Protecting the wound",html:"<p>Licking and chewing are the biggest threats to a healing wound, they introduce bacteria and can pull stitches. The traditional plastic cone works but many dogs hate it.</p><ul><li>A soft recovery suit covers the wound and is far comfier than a cone</li><li>Keep the area clean and dry, and check it daily</li><li>Watch for redness, swelling, discharge or a bad smell</li><li>Don\u2019t let your dog scratch or rub the site on furniture or flooring</li></ul>"},{h:"Rest, comfort and gentle progress",html:"<p>Most complications come from doing too much, too soon. Restricting activity is often the hardest part, but it\u2019s essential.</p><ul><li>Provide soft, supportive bedding in a quiet, warm spot</li><li>Restrict activity exactly as your vet advises, no jumping or stairs</li><li>Use ramps and a support harness to avoid strain</li><li>Keep to short, calm toilet breaks on the lead</li><li>Follow the medication schedule and attend all check-ups</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Stitches","Healing wound","Cone-free care","Rest"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Contact your vet if the wound looks red, swollen, hot, smelly or is leaking, if stitches come loose, or if your dog seems unusually painful, off their food or lethargic. Quick action prevents small problems becoming big ones.",shop:{h:"Shop recovery & wound care",href:"/collections/recovery-immunity"},faqTitle:"Post-Surgery Recovery for Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"How long until my dog is back to normal?",a:"It depends on the procedure, from around 10 to 14 days for routine surgery to several weeks or months for orthopaedic operations. Follow your vet\u2019s timeline and don\u2019t rush it."},{q:"Is a recovery suit better than a cone?",a:"Many dogs find a soft recovery suit much less stressful than a cone, and it still protects the wound. It\u2019s a great option for body and limb wounds."},{q:"How do I stop my dog licking the wound?",a:"A recovery suit or cone, keeping them occupied and calm, and addressing any itch as it heals all help. Never ignore persistent licking."}],related:["ivdd-in-dogs","cruciate-ligament-injury-dogs"]},"dog-anxiety-and-stress":{title:"Dog Anxiety and Stress: Signs and Calming Strategies",cat:"Behaviour & Mood",catKey:"behaviour",meta:["8 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Anxiety is one of the most common behavioural issues in dogs, and one of the most misunderstood. A stressed dog isn\u2019t being \u201Cnaughty\u201D; they\u2019re struggling to cope. Recognising the signs and building a sense of safety can transform their quality of life.",sections:[{h:"What causes anxiety in dogs?",html:"<p>Common triggers include being left alone (separation anxiety), loud noises like fireworks and thunder, changes in routine or environment, and a lack of early socialisation. Some dogs are simply more sensitive by nature. Whatever the cause, the underlying feeling is the same: they don\u2019t feel safe.</p>"},{h:"Recognising the signs",html:"<p>Anxiety shows up in lots of ways, and the signs are easy to mistake for other things. Look for patterns around specific triggers.</p><ul><li>Pacing, restlessness or an inability to settle</li><li>Whining, barking or howling, often when left alone</li><li>Destructive behaviour, chewing or toileting indoors</li><li>Trembling, hiding or becoming unusually clingy</li><li>Excessive panting, drooling or yawning when not hot or tired</li></ul>"},{h:"Helping an anxious dog feel calmer",html:"<p>There\u2019s no instant fix, but a combination of routine, environment and support makes a big difference over time. Consistency and patience are everything.</p><ul><li>Keep a predictable daily routine, it builds security</li><li>Create a quiet, cosy \u2018safe space\u2019 your dog can retreat to</li><li>Use calming aids such as supplements or a pressure vest for tricky moments</li><li>Build up alone-time gradually rather than all at once</li><li>Reward calm behaviour and avoid punishment, which increases fear</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Pacing","Whining alone","Trembling","Destructive"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Speak to your vet or a qualified behaviourist if anxiety is severe, getting worse, or affecting your dog\u2019s quality of life. They can rule out underlying pain and, in some cases, recommend a behaviour programme or medication that supportive aids work alongside.",shop:{h:"Shop calming & anxiety support",href:"/collections/calm-anxiety"},faqTitle:"Dog Anxiety and Stress, answered",faq:[{q:"How can I help with separation anxiety?",a:"Build up time alone very gradually, make departures and returns low-key, leave them with something comforting, and consider calming support. Severe cases benefit from a structured plan."},{q:"Do calming chews work?",a:"Calming supplements can help take the edge off, especially around predictable triggers like fireworks. They work best alongside a consistent routine and environment."},{q:"Will my dog grow out of it?",a:"Anxiety rarely resolves on its own, but with the right support and, where needed, professional help, most dogs can learn to cope far better."}],related:["senior-dog-care-guide","dog-arthritis-signs-and-support"]},"dental-disease-in-dogs":{title:"Dental Disease in Dogs: Bad Breath, Tartar and Daily Care",cat:"Essential Care",catKey:"essential",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Bad breath isn\u2019t just unpleasant, it\u2019s usually the first sign of dental disease, which affects the majority of dogs by middle age. Left unchecked it causes pain and can affect overall health. The good news is that a simple daily routine goes a long way.",sections:[{h:"Why dental health matters",html:"<p>Plaque builds up on the teeth, hardens into tartar, and irritates the gums. Over time this leads to gum disease, painful teeth and, in advanced cases, bacteria that can affect other organs. Because dogs hide oral pain so well, problems often go unnoticed until they\u2019re advanced.</p>"},{h:"Signs of dental disease",html:"<p>Lift your dog\u2019s lip and take a look every so often, early changes are easy to miss.</p><ul><li>Bad breath (often the earliest sign)</li><li>Yellow or brown tartar along the gum line</li><li>Red, swollen or bleeding gums</li><li>Dropping food, chewing on one side or eating more slowly</li><li>Pawing at the mouth or reluctance to have the face touched</li></ul>"},{h:"Building a dental routine",html:"<p>Prevention is far easier than treatment. A little and often is the secret to keeping teeth healthy between professional cleans.</p><ul><li>Brush the teeth with dog-specific toothpaste, daily is ideal</li><li>Introduce brushing gradually and make it positive</li><li>Use dental chews and toys to help reduce plaque</li><li>Check the mouth regularly so you spot changes early</li><li>Book routine dental check-ups with your vet</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Bad breath","Tartar","Sore gums","Dropping food"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if you notice heavy tartar, red or bleeding gums, loose or broken teeth, or signs of mouth pain. Established tartar usually needs a professional scale and polish, after which a home routine keeps things healthy.",shop:{h:"Shop dental care",href:"/collections/dental-care"},faqTitle:"Dental Disease in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"How often should I brush my dog\u2019s teeth?",a:"Daily is ideal, but even a few times a week makes a real difference. Consistency matters more than perfection."},{q:"Do dental chews replace brushing?",a:"They\u2019re a great addition and help reduce plaque, but they don\u2019t fully replace brushing. Use both for best results."},{q:"Can I use human toothpaste?",a:"No, human toothpaste isn\u2019t safe for dogs. Always use a toothpaste made specifically for dogs."}],related:["senior-dog-care-guide","dog-anxiety-and-stress"]},"dog-digestive-issues-diarrhoea-sensitive-stomachs-and-gut-health":{title:"Dog Digestive Issues: Diarrhoea, Sensitive Stomachs and Gut Health",cat:"Internal Health",catKey:"internal",meta:["7 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"An occasional upset tummy is part of life with a dog. But frequent diarrhoea, vomiting, wind or appetite changes can be a sign your dog\u2019s digestion needs some support. Here\u2019s how to help a sensitive stomach and keep their gut healthy.",sections:[{h:"Common causes of tummy trouble",html:"<p>Dogs have famously indiscriminate appetites, so dietary indiscretion (eating something they shouldn\u2019t) is a frequent cause. Other triggers include sudden diet changes, stress, food sensitivities, parasites and an imbalance in the gut\u2019s natural bacteria.</p>"},{h:"Signs of digestive problems",html:"<p>Keep an eye on consistency, frequency and your dog\u2019s general demeanour, ongoing changes are more telling than a one-off.</p><ul><li>Loose stools or diarrhoea</li><li>Vomiting or regurgitation</li><li>A gurgling, noisy stomach</li><li>Excessive wind</li><li>Changes in appetite or energy</li></ul>"},{h:"Supporting healthy digestion",html:"<p>A settled gut comes down to consistency, gentle support and not overloading the system. Small changes often make a big difference.</p><ul><li>Introduce any diet changes gradually over a week or so</li><li>Stick to a consistent feeding routine and avoid too many rich treats</li><li>Consider a probiotic to support healthy gut bacteria</li><li>Make sure fresh water is always available</li><li>Keep a simple food-and-symptom diary to spot patterns</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Loose stools","Wind","Appetite changes","Noisy tummy"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Contact your vet if diarrhoea or vomiting lasts more than a couple of days, contains blood, or your dog seems lethargic, painful or won\u2019t eat or drink, especially in puppies and seniors, who can dehydrate quickly.",shop:{h:"Shop digestion & gut support",href:"/collections/digestion"},faqTitle:"Dog Digestive Issues, answered",faq:[{q:"Do probiotics help dogs?",a:"Probiotics can help restore the balance of good gut bacteria, which supports digestion and stool quality, useful after upsets, antibiotics or diet changes."},{q:"Should I withhold food if my dog has diarrhoea?",a:"Approaches vary, so it\u2019s best to check with your vet. Often a short period on a bland, easily digestible diet helps things settle."},{q:"Why does my dog have a sensitive stomach?",a:"Some dogs are simply more prone to it. Consistent feeding, a suitable diet and gut support usually keep things much steadier."}],related:["itchy-skin-allergies-in-dogs","senior-dog-care-guide","dog-anxiety-and-stress"]},"hot-spots-in-dogs-how-to-treat-and-prevent-them":{title:"Hot Spots in Dogs: How to Treat and Prevent Them",cat:"Skin & Allergies",catKey:"skin",meta:["5 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"A hot spot (acute moist dermatitis) is a red, sore, often weepy patch of skin that can appear seemingly out of nowhere and grow alarmingly fast. They\u2019re uncomfortable and itchy, and the more your dog licks, the worse they get. Here\u2019s how to break the cycle.",sections:[{h:"What causes hot spots?",html:"<p>Hot spots start when something irritates the skin, an insect bite, an allergy, moisture trapped in the coat, or a small graze. The dog licks and scratches the area, which inflames it further, creating the perfect conditions for the patch to spread and sometimes become infected.</p>"},{h:"How to recognise a hot spot",html:"<p>They tend to appear suddenly and are easy to spot once you know what you\u2019re looking for.</p><ul><li>A red, moist, angry-looking patch of skin</li><li>Hair loss over and around the area</li><li>Constant licking, chewing or scratching of one spot</li><li>Sometimes an unpleasant smell or discharge</li></ul>"},{h:"Soothing and preventing hot spots",html:"<p>The priorities are to calm the skin and stop your dog making it worse. Catching them early makes a big difference.</p><ul><li>Gently clip and clean the area so it can breathe and dry out</li><li>Apply a soothing balm to calm the skin</li><li>Stop the licking, a recovery suit is a comfier alternative to a cone</li><li>Address the underlying trigger, often allergies or fleas</li><li>Keep the coat clean and fully dry, especially after swimming or baths</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Raw patch","Licking one spot","Hair loss","Odour"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if the hot spot is large, deep, very painful, smelly, or not improving within a day or two, these often need antibiotics or anti-inflammatories alongside good home care.",shop:{h:"Shop skin soothing & recovery",href:"/collections/skin-coat"},faqTitle:"Hot Spots in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"How quickly do hot spots spread?",a:"Very quickly, sometimes within hours, because licking and scratching keeps making them worse. Early action is key."},{q:"Will a recovery suit help?",a:"Yes. Stopping your dog reaching the area is half the battle, and a soft recovery suit is far more comfortable than a plastic cone."},{q:"How do I stop them coming back?",a:"Tackle the underlying cause (often allergies or fleas), keep the coat clean and dry, and act fast at the first sign of licking one spot."}],related:["itchy-skin-allergies-in-dogs","post-surgery-recovery-for-dogs"]},"itchy-skin-allergies-in-dogs":{title:"Itchy Skin and Allergies in Dogs: Causes and Soothing Solutions",cat:"Skin & Allergies",catKey:"skin",meta:["8 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Few things are more frustrating than a dog that just won\u2019t stop scratching. Itchy skin is one of the most common reasons dogs visit the vet, and it has many possible causes. Here\u2019s how to work out what\u2019s behind it and soothe your dog\u2019s skin.",sections:[{h:"Why is my dog so itchy?",html:"<p>Itching (the medical term is pruritus) is a symptom, not a diagnosis. The most common culprits are allergies, to fleas, food, or things in the environment like pollen and dust mites, along with dry skin, irritation and skin infections that develop from all the scratching.</p>"},{h:"Signs of itchy, irritated skin",html:"<p>Beyond the obvious scratching, look for the knock-on effects on the skin and coat.</p><ul><li>Frequent scratching, licking, chewing or rubbing</li><li>Red, inflamed or broken skin</li><li>Hair loss or thinning patches</li><li>Flaky, dry or smelly skin</li><li>Recurring hot spots or ear problems</li></ul>"},{h:"Soothing and managing itchy skin",html:"<p>A calm, consistent skin-care routine helps break the itch-scratch cycle. The aim is to soothe the skin, support the skin barrier and reduce exposure to triggers.</p><ul><li>Stay on top of flea prevention all year round</li><li>Use gentle, soothing topical products on irritated areas</li><li>Support skin and coat from the inside with omega oils</li><li>Wipe paws and coat after walks in allergy season</li><li>Keep bedding clean and consider what\u2019s in their diet</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Scratching","Red skin","Licking","Flaky coat"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if the skin is broken, smelly, very red, or if the itching is intense and persistent. Allergies and infections often need a proper diagnosis and treatment plan, supportive products then help you maintain healthy skin day to day.",shop:{h:"Shop skin & coat care",href:"/collections/skin-coat"},faqTitle:"Itchy Skin and Allergies in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Could it be a food allergy?",a:"Possibly. Food allergies are less common than environmental ones but do happen. Your vet may suggest a carefully managed diet trial to find out."},{q:"Do omega oils help itchy skin?",a:"Omega-3 and -6 fatty acids support the skin barrier and coat condition, and can help reduce dryness and irritation as part of a wider plan."},{q:"Why does my dog get itchy at certain times of year?",a:"Seasonal itching usually points to environmental allergens like pollen. Wiping the coat after walks and a consistent skin routine can really help."}],related:["senior-dog-care-guide"]},"dog-arthritis-signs-and-support":{title:"Dog Arthritis: Signs, Stages and How to Help at Home",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["8 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Arthritis (osteoarthritis) affects a huge number of dogs as they age, and many more after an injury. The good news: while it can't be cured, it can be managed really well at home with the right routine and support. Here's how to spot it early and help your dog stay comfortable and mobile.",sections:[{h:"What is arthritis in dogs?",html:"<p>Arthritis is the gradual wearing of the smooth cartilage that cushions a joint. As that cushioning thins, bones move less smoothly, the joint becomes inflamed, and movement starts to hurt. It most often affects the hips, knees, elbows and spine.</p><p>It tends to creep in slowly, which is why so many owners put the early signs down to \u201Cjust getting older.\u201D Spotting it sooner means you can slow its progression and keep your dog active for longer.</p>"},{h:"Early signs to watch for",html:"<p>Arthritis pain is rarely dramatic, dogs are masters at hiding discomfort. Look for subtle, consistent changes in how your dog moves and behaves, especially first thing in the morning or after a long rest.</p><ul><li>Stiffness when getting up, that eases once they\u2019re warmed up</li><li>Slowing down or lagging behind on walks</li><li>Reluctance to jump into the car, onto the sofa or up stairs</li><li>Licking or nibbling at a specific joint</li><li>A change in mood, grumpier, quieter or less playful</li></ul>"},{h:"What makes arthritis worse?",html:"<p>Excess weight is the single biggest factor you can control, every extra kilo puts more load through already-sore joints. Cold, damp weather, hard or slippery flooring, and bursts of high-impact exercise (like chasing a ball) can all trigger flare-ups too.</p>"},{h:"How to help your arthritic dog at home",html:"<p>Management is about lots of small, consistent changes rather than one big fix. The aim is to reduce strain on the joints, keep muscles strong, and ease day-to-day discomfort.</p><ul><li>Keep them lean, even a small amount of weight loss makes a noticeable difference</li><li>Swap one big walk for several shorter, gentle ones to avoid over-doing it</li><li>Add grip, rugs or runners over slippery floors prevent painful slips</li><li>Use a supportive orthopaedic bed to cushion joints overnight</li><li>Consider joint supplements and a ramp for the car or sofa</li><li>Keep them warm and dry, especially after walks in winter</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Stiff after rest","Slowing on walks","Reluctant to jump","Licking a joint"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> Book a vet check if your dog is suddenly very lame, won\u2019t bear weight on a leg, cries out in pain, or deteriorates quickly. Your vet can confirm arthritis, rule out other causes and discuss pain relief that our supportive products can sit alongside.",shop:{h:"Shop joint & mobility support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Dog Arthritis, answered",faq:[{q:"At what age do dogs get arthritis?",a:"It\u2019s most common in middle-aged and senior dogs, but it can affect dogs of any age, particularly larger breeds, or any dog that\u2019s had a joint injury or a condition like hip dysplasia."},{q:"Can arthritis in dogs be reversed?",a:"No, the cartilage damage can\u2019t be undone, but its progression can be slowed and the pain managed very effectively. Many dogs go on to live happy, active lives with the right routine."},{q:"Do joint supplements actually work?",a:"Supplements support joint health as part of a wider plan, alongside weight management, sensible exercise and anything your vet prescribes. Give them time; most are designed to build up over several weeks."}],related:["senior-dog-care-guide","cruciate-ligament-injury-dogs"]},"hip-dysplasia-in-dogs-symptoms-support-and-day-to-day-management":{title:"Hip Dysplasia in Dogs: Symptoms, Support and Day-to-Day Management",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["7 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"Hip dysplasia is one of the most common skeletal conditions in dogs, particularly larger and fast-growing breeds. It happens when the hip joint doesn\u2019t fit together snugly, leading to looseness, wear and, over time, arthritis. With the right support, most dogs manage very well.",sections:[{h:"What is hip dysplasia?",html:"<p>A healthy hip is a tight ball-and-socket joint. In hip dysplasia, the \u201Cfit\u201D is loose, so the joint rubs and grinds instead of gliding. That looseness causes inflammation and, eventually, secondary arthritis. It\u2019s largely genetic but can be made worse by rapid growth, over-exercise in puppyhood and excess weight.</p>"},{h:"Common symptoms",html:"<p>Signs can appear in young dogs as they grow, or later in life as arthritis sets in. Many owners first notice a distinctive way of moving.</p><ul><li>A \u201Cbunny-hopping\u201D run, using both back legs together</li><li>A swaying or wobbly back end</li><li>Difficulty rising, especially after rest</li><li>Reluctance to climb stairs or jump up</li><li>Reduced muscle over the hips and thighs</li></ul>"},{h:"Managing hip dysplasia at home",html:"<p>The goals are to protect the joint, keep the supporting muscles strong, and control pain. Consistency matters more than intensity.</p><ul><li>Keep your dog lean, this is the most important single factor</li><li>Favour low-impact exercise like lead walks and swimming over jumping and sprinting</li><li>Use a rear support harness to help with stairs, cars and slippery floors</li><li>Provide a firm orthopaedic bed and warm, draught-free rest areas</li><li>Build a gentle, regular routine rather than \u2018weekend warrior\u2019 bursts</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Bunny-hopping","Swaying gait","Trouble rising","Won\u2019t climb stairs"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet if your dog is in obvious pain, suddenly much worse, or struggling to stand. They can grade the hips, manage pain and advise whether physiotherapy, hydrotherapy or surgery may help in more severe cases.",shop:{h:"Shop mobility support & harnesses",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Hip Dysplasia in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Which breeds are most at risk?",a:"Larger breeds such as Labradors, German Shepherds, Golden Retrievers and Rottweilers are more prone, but smaller breeds can be affected too."},{q:"Can a dog live a normal life with hip dysplasia?",a:"Yes, many dogs do really well with weight control, sensible exercise, supportive aids and pain management. The key is starting good habits early."},{q:"Does a harness help?",a:"A well-fitted rear support harness takes pressure off the hips and gives you a gentle way to assist with stairs, getting up and into the car."}],related:["dog-arthritis-signs-and-support","rear-leg-weakness-in-dogs","senior-dog-care-guide"]},"ivdd-in-dogs":{title:"IVDD in Dogs: What It Is and How to Support Recovery",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["7 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"IVDD (Intervertebral Disc Disease) affects the cushioning discs between the bones of the spine. It\u2019s especially common in long-backed breeds like Dachshunds, and can range from mild back pain to a sudden, serious emergency. Knowing the signs, and acting quickly, makes a real difference.",sections:[{h:"Understanding IVDD",html:"<p>The discs in your dog\u2019s spine act like shock absorbers. With IVDD, a disc bulges or ruptures and presses on the spinal cord. Depending on where and how badly, that can cause anything from discomfort to loss of movement in the legs. It can come on gradually or very suddenly.</p>"},{h:"Warning signs",html:"<p>IVDD can escalate quickly, so treat these signs seriously, particularly any sudden change in your dog\u2019s back legs.</p><ul><li>Yelping or flinching when touched or picked up</li><li>A hunched or arched back and a reluctance to move</li><li>Wobbliness, scuffing or weakness in the back legs</li><li>Reluctance to jump, climb stairs or be lifted</li><li>In severe cases, dragging the back legs or being unable to stand</li></ul>"},{h:"Supporting recovery at home",html:"<p>Whether your dog is managed with rest or has had surgery, the recovery phase is where supportive care really counts. Always follow your vet\u2019s specific plan first, these tips sit alongside it.</p><ul><li>Strict, comfortable crate rest when advised, it\u2019s often the hardest but most important part</li><li>Prevent jumping and stairs with ramps, pens and barriers</li><li>A supportive harness to help with toileting and short, controlled movement</li><li>Soft, supportive bedding and help keeping clean and comfortable</li><li>Patience, recovery is measured in weeks, not days</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Yelping","Hunched back","Wobbly back legs","Won\u2019t jump"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> IVDD can be an emergency. Contact your vet straight away if your dog suddenly can\u2019t walk, drags a leg, loses control of toileting, or is in severe pain, fast treatment greatly improves the outlook.",shop:{h:"Shop back support & recovery aids",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"IVDD in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Which dogs get IVDD?",a:"Long-backed breeds like Dachshunds, Basset Hounds, Beagles and French Bulldogs are most prone, but any dog can be affected."},{q:"Can a dog recover from IVDD without surgery?",a:"Many milder cases respond well to strict rest and medication. More severe cases may need surgery. Your vet will advise based on the severity."},{q:"How can I prevent flare-ups?",a:"Keep your dog lean, discourage jumping on and off furniture, use ramps, and support the back when lifting (support both the chest and the rear)."}],related:["dog-arthritis-signs-and-support","rear-leg-weakness-in-dogs","post-surgery-recovery-for-dogs"]},"cruciate-ligament-injury-dogs":{title:"Cruciate Ligament Injuries (ACL/CCL) in Dogs",cat:"Mobility & Joint",catKey:"mobility",meta:["6 min read","Vet-informed guidance"],lead:"The cruciate ligament stabilises your dog\u2019s knee (stifle) joint. When it\u2019s strained or torn, often during a twist, jump or sudden turn, it causes pain and lameness in a back leg. It\u2019s one of the most common orthopaedic injuries vets see.",sections:[{h:"What is a cruciate injury?",html:"<p>Dogs have a ligament inside the knee (the cranial cruciate, equivalent to the human ACL) that stops the joint sliding around. It can rupture suddenly during activity, or wear and partially tear over time. Once injured, the knee becomes unstable and inflamed.</p>"},{h:"Signs of a cruciate problem",html:"<p>The classic picture is a dog that\u2019s suddenly lame on a back leg, often after exercise or play.</p><ul><li>Sudden limping or holding a back leg up</li><li>Stiffness, especially after resting</li><li>Sitting with one leg stuck out to the side</li><li>Swelling around the knee</li><li>Reluctance to bear full weight on the leg</li></ul>"},{h:"Supporting the knee at home",html:"<p>Cruciate injuries often need veterinary assessment and sometimes surgery, but supportive care helps comfort and recovery either way.</p><ul><li>Rest and restricted activity while it\u2019s assessed and healing</li><li>A knee brace or support to stabilise the joint where appropriate</li><li>Weight control to reduce load on the knee</li><li>Non-slip flooring and ramps to avoid twists and slips</li><li>Gradual, controlled return to exercise as guided by your vet</li></ul>"}],signs:{h:"Signs to look for",items:["Sudden limp","Holding leg up","Stiff after rest","Swollen knee"]},vet:"<b>When to see your vet.</b> See your vet for any sudden hind-leg lameness. They can examine the knee for instability, take imaging if needed, and advise on whether management or surgery is the best route for your dog.",shop:{h:"Shop knee & joint support",href:"/collections/joints-mobility"},faqTitle:"Cruciate Ligament Injuries (ACL/CCL) in Dogs, answered",faq:[{q:"Do all cruciate tears need surgery?",a:"Not always, smaller dogs and partial tears can sometimes be managed conservatively. Larger, active dogs often do best with surgery. Your vet will advise."},{q:"Can a knee brace help?",a:"A supportive brace can help stabilise the joint and aid comfort, particularly during conservative management or recovery, alongside rest and rehab."},{q:"How long is recovery?",a:"It varies, but expect several weeks to a few months of controlled, gradually increasing activity. Rushing it risks re-injury."}],related:["dog-arthritis-signs-and-support","post-surgery-recovery-for-dogs"]}}};
